chd:Document a doc:ReportSection ; rdfs:label "Flow control for an advanced course: chapter 2 of the 3rd edition and today's Python"@en ; skos:definition "This document renews chapter 2 of the 3rd edition for an advanced course, pairing the book's Boolean logic and decisions with the Python students now run (Sweigart, 2025)." ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02/research> ;
    doc:hasSection chd:S_Value, chd:S_Comparison, chd:S_BooleanOperation, chd:S_FlowControl, chd:S_ModernPractice, chd:S_WorkedProgram, chd:S_ErrorReport .

chd:S_Value a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Value"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Value" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 1 ;
    skos:definition "Chapter 1 introduced the value as a piece of data that a program can compute with, and it named three kinds of value: integers, floating-point numbers and strings. Chapter 2 adds a fourth kind, the Boolean value, which has only two members, True and False, and which answers a yes-or-no question (Sweigart, 2025). This branch of the chapter collects what concerns such values: what they are, how Python spells them, and how any other value can be asked whether it counts as true or as false.\n\nA program that only calculates and prints follows the same path every time it runs. Real programs choose: they show one message to a child and another to an adult, or they accept a password and refuse another. A choice needs a question with exactly two possible answers, and the Boolean value is the answer to such a question. Almost every later chapter depends on it, because loops, searches and error handling all continue or stop according to a true or false value.\n\nBoolean values appear as the result of every comparison, as the operands of the operators and, or and not, and as the condition that follows the word if. They also appear in less obvious places. Python lets any value, not only True and False, stand where a true or false value is expected, so that an empty string or the number zero counts as false while nearly everything else counts as true (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nThe branch has four sections. The Boolean value is the strict case, with exactly two members. Truthiness widens the idea so that any value can be tested. The value None, which stands for the absence of a value, and the sequences and collections, which can be empty, are the two groups of values whose truth is needed most often. Later branches of the chapter show how comparisons produce these values, how Boolean operators combine them and how flow control statements use them to choose which instructions run.\n\nThe two Boolean values must be written exactly as Python spells them, with a capital first letter and without quotation marks. The word true in lowercase is not a Boolean value, and the text 'True' in quotation marks is a string, which Python treats as a different value (Sweigart, 2025)." ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#Value> .
chd:S_TruthValue a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Truth value"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Truth value" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 2 ;
    skos:definition "Truth values are the values that answer a yes-or-no question. They include the two Boolean values True and False, the special value None that stands for the absence of a value, and the rule by which every other value can also be read as true or false. This second level of the branch gathers the ideas that a learner needs before comparisons and decisions make sense: the Boolean type, the notion of truthiness, the value None and the sequences and collections whose emptiness decides their truth.\n\nTruth is the currency of flow control, because every decision that a program makes reduces to a true or false value (Sweigart, 2025). A program can only be read correctly by someone who knows which values count as true. A condition does not have to be a Boolean value, since Python accepts any value in its place and decides for itself whether it is true (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nThe sections that follow go from the narrow to the wide. The Boolean value is the strict case. Truthiness widens it to every value that Python can test. The value None and the sequences and collections come last, because they are the values whose truth a program most often needs to read, and the last section explains what a sequence or a collection is, since the earlier sections have to use those words.\n\nThe widening is written into the language's own types. The two Boolean values are whole numbers as well, so True == 1 and False == 0 both evaluate to True and isinstance(True, int) evaluates to True, because bool is defined as a subclass of int (Python Software Foundation, 2026). Truthiness then covers everything else: bool(0), bool(''), bool([]) and bool(None) all evaluate to False, while bool(0.1) and bool('False') both evaluate to True, which is why a string that reads False is nevertheless a true value." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_Value ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#TruthValue> .
chd:S_BooleanValue a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Boolean value"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Boolean value" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 3 ;
    skos:definition "A Boolean value is a value of the data type bool, and that type has exactly two members, True and False. Where the integer, floating-point and string types have an unlimited number of possible values, the Boolean type has only these two (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation states the same fact by saying that the bool type has exactly two constant instances, True and False (Python Software Foundation, 2026); an instance of a type is simply one of its objects, so there are exactly two Boolean objects.\n\nTwo members are exactly what a yes-or-no question needs. Every decision that a program makes, however elaborate its reasons, comes down to one of these two answers, and the flow control statements of this chapter do nothing except choose between instructions according to which answer they receive. The type is named after the mathematician George Boole, which explains why both the name of the type and its two values begin with a capital letter (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nBoolean values are rarely typed in directly; they mostly arrive as results. The comparison 42 == 42 evaluates to True, the comparison 42 == 99 evaluates to False, and the operators and, or and not produce Boolean values from other Boolean values. A Boolean value can also be stored in a variable like any other value, as in spam = True, and the textbook enters exactly that line in the interactive shell (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nIn source code the two values are written True and False, each with a capital first letter, without quotation marks and with the rest of the word in lowercase (Sweigart, 2025). They are keywords of the language, an idea that the section on keywords explains, and for that reason an assignment to either of them is illegal and raises a SyntaxError (Python Software Foundation, 2026). Under Python 3.14 the line False = 2 + 2 is rejected with the message cannot assign to False. The type bool is also a subtype of the integer type, so Boolean values behave like 0 and 1 in almost all contexts, the exception being conversion to text (Python Software Foundation, 2026): True + True evaluates to 2 and int(True) evaluates to 1, while str(True) gives the text 'True'.\n\nThe capital letter is not a matter of style. The lowercase word true is not a Boolean value but an unknown name, and entering it produces a NameError with the message name 'true' is not defined. Quotation marks change the meaning just as much: the string 'True' is text, and the comparison 'True' == True evaluates to False. Because True counts as 1, an expression such as True + True is legal, but it is rarely what a reader expects and is best avoided." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_TruthValue ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#BooleanValue> .
chd:S_Truthiness a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Truthiness"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Truthiness" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 4 ;
    skos:definition "Truthiness is the property of every Python value of being counted as true or as false when a Boolean value is needed. The documentation states the principle directly: any object can be tested for truth value, for use in a condition or as an operand of the Boolean operations (Python Software Foundation, 2026). A value that Python counts as true is therefore not necessarily True, and a value that it counts as false is not necessarily False.\n\nWithout this rule a program would have to compare each value with something before it could decide anything, for example by asking whether a name equals the empty string. With the rule the value itself can serve as the test, so that a program can ask directly whether the user typed anything, whether a result was found or whether a group of items has any members. The style guide of the language relies on exactly this when it recommends the plain test over a comparison with True (Van Rossum et al., 2001).\n\nTruthiness operates wherever Python expects a condition: after the word if, as an operand of and, or and not, and in the built-in function bool, which converts any value to True or False (Python Software Foundation, 2026). It is the reason that a line such as if name: runs its block for a non-empty string and skips it for an empty one.\n\nBy default an object is considered true unless it belongs to a short list of exceptions (Python Software Foundation, 2026). The values counted as false are the constants None and False, zero of any numeric type, such as 0 and 0.0, and the empty sequences and collections '', (), [], {} and set(). Everything else is true. The function bool shows the rule at work: bool('') evaluates to False, bool(0.0) evaluates to False, and bool(' ') evaluates to True because a string that holds one space is not empty.\n\nTruth is decided by emptiness or zero, never by what the content says. The string '0' and the string 'False' are both non-empty and therefore true: bool('0') evaluates to True and bool('False') evaluates to True. A list that contains a zero is not empty either, so bool([0]) evaluates to True. Truthiness is also not equality: the empty list counts as false when it is tested, yet the comparison [] == False evaluates to False because a list and a Boolean value are different values. Programmers who define their own kinds of object can decide how those objects are read, which is a subject for later chapters." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_TruthValue ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#Truthiness> .
chd:S_NoneValue a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "None value"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "None value" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 5 ;
    skos:definition "None is a built-in value that represents the absence of a value. The documentation describes it as an object frequently used to represent the absence of a value, and as the sole instance of the type NoneType, which means that it is the only object of that type (Python Software Foundation, 2026). It is written None, with a capital N and without quotation marks, in the same way as True and False.\n\nA program often has to say that nothing is there yet without inventing a number or a string for the purpose. Zero and the empty string already mean something, so they make poor markers of absence, and None is a distinct value that cannot be mistaken for them. A variable that has not yet received a real answer can hold None, and a function that finds nothing can hand it back.\n\nNone is what a function produces when it does not return anything explicitly; the language reference says it is returned from functions that don't explicitly return anything (Python Software Foundation, 2026). The function print is such a function, so the expression print() is None evaluates to True, after printing an empty line. None is also the value that conditions on missing data are written against, as in the test x is None.\n\nThere is exactly one None in a running program. The call type(None) reports <class 'NoneType'>, and the language reference states that its truth value is false (Python Software Foundation, 2026), so bool(None) evaluates to False and the expression None or 'default' evaluates to 'default'. Because the value is unique, the program can recognise it by identity, with the operator is, which the section on identity comparison explains.\n\nNone is false, but it is not equal to False and not equal to zero: None == False evaluates to False and None == 0 evaluates to False. The style guide asks that comparisons with None be made with is or is not and never with the equality operators (Van Rossum et al., 2001). It also warns against writing if x when the intention was if x is not None, because the other value might be a container that happens to be empty, and an empty container counts as false (Van Rossum et al., 2001)." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_TruthValue ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#NoneValue> .
chd:S_Container a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Sequence and collection"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Sequence and collection" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 6 ;
    skos:definition "A sequence is an ordered group of items that can be reached by position and that has a length. The string of Chapter 1 is a sequence of characters, and the glossary names list, str, tuple and bytes (a sequence of byte values) as the built-in sequence types (Python Software Foundation, 2026). A list is written with square brackets, as in [10, 20, 30], and a tuple is written with parentheses, as in (10, 20, 30). A collection is the wider word for any object that holds other values, so it also covers the dictionary, which maps keys to values, and the set, which holds each distinct item once and has no order (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nThe chapter needs these words because the rule for truth refers to them: empty sequences and collections count as false. A program often needs to know whether a group has any members at all, for example whether a search found anything or whether the user entered a value, and the emptiness of a group is the simplest way to say so.\n\nSequences and collections are everywhere in real programs, and later chapters of the course treat lists and dictionaries in detail. In this chapter they matter in two places: when the truth of a value is tested, and when the match statement compares a value against patterns that describe a sequence.\n\nThe function len from Chapter 1 reports the number of items, so len([10, 20, 30]) evaluates to 3 and len('') evaluates to 0. An empty group is written '' for a string, [] for a list, () for a tuple, set() for a set and {} for a dictionary: empty braces create a dictionary, and type({}) reports <class 'dict'>, which is why the empty set needs a function call of its own. Each of these empty values counts as false.\n\nA group counts as false only when it is empty. A group of one item is true even if that item is itself false or empty: bool([0]) evaluates to True and bool([[]]) evaluates to True, because the outer list holds one item. The truth of a group says nothing about the truth of its members." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_TruthValue ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#Container> .
chd:S_Comparison a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Comparison"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Comparison" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 7 ;
    skos:definition "A comparison is an expression that asks a question about two values and answers with a Boolean value. The textbook calls the operators that write such questions comparison operators or, by a second name, relational operators, and it lists six of them: ==, !=, <, >, <= and >= (Sweigart, 2025). The reference documentation counts eight comparison operations, because it adds the two identity operators is and is not to the six (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nA program cannot decide anything without information about its data, and comparisons are how data is turned into the true-or-false answer that a decision needs. They ask whether a name equals the expected one, whether an age lies below a limit, or whether two variables hold the same value. Comparing two literal values, that is, two values written directly in the program such as 'dog' and 'cat', is rarely useful, because the answer is always the same; comparisons earn their place when at least one side is a variable (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nComparisons are written in the condition of every if and elif statement, and they are the usual operands of the Boolean operators and, or and not. The examples of the chapter compare a name with 'Alice', an age with 12, and a stored number with 42. A comparison is an expression like any other, so its value can also be printed or stored in a variable.\n\nEvery comparison operator takes two operands and evaluates to True or False (Sweigart, 2025). All comparison operators have the same priority, and that priority is lower than the priority of any arithmetic operator (Python Software Foundation, 2026). In 2 + 2 == 4 the addition is therefore evaluated first, and the comparison then asks whether 4 equals 4. The branch is divided into equality comparisons, which ask whether two values are the same, and ordering comparisons, which ask which of two values comes first.\n\nA comparison produces a Boolean value, not a decision: nothing happens until a flow control statement acts on the result. Unless stated otherwise, objects of different types never compare equal (Python Software Foundation, 2026), but the numeric types are an exception, so 42 == 42.0 evaluates to True while 42 == '42' evaluates to False." ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#Comparison> .
chd:S_EqualityComparison a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Equality comparison"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Equality comparison" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 8 ;
    skos:definition "Equality comparisons ask whether two values are the same. The two operators for the purpose are == and !=, which compare the values of their operands, and the identity operators is and is not, which ask the different question of whether two expressions refer to one and the same object (Python Software Foundation, 2026). The branch also treats the frequent confusion between the equality operator == and the assignment operator =.\n\nEquality is the question that programs ask most often: whether the user typed the expected word, whether a number reached an exact target, whether a value is the special value None. The three questions look similar and need three different tools, and choosing the wrong one produces programs that run but answer incorrectly.\n\nThe sections of the branch take the value comparison first, then the difference between == and =, and finally the identity comparison. The first two use the textbook's own examples; the third comes from the style guide, which asks that some comparisons be made with is (Van Rossum et al., 2001).\n\nThe three questions come apart on real values, and each failure looks different. The comparison [1, 2] == [1, 2] evaluates to True while [1, 2] is [1, 2] evaluates to False, because the two lists hold the same items in two different objects (Python Software Foundation, 2026). Writing one equals sign where two were meant is caught earlier still: the line if spam = 42: never runs at all, since Python refuses the file with a SyntaxError whose message asks whether == or := was meant. Only the first two mistakes can be found by reading what a program printed." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_Comparison ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#EqualityComparison> .
chd:S_Equality a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Equality"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Equality" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 9 ;
    skos:definition "The equality operators compare two values. The operator == (equal to) evaluates to True when the values on both sides are the same, and the operator != (not equal to) evaluates to True when the two values are different (Sweigart, 2025). Both operators return a Boolean value and neither changes the values it compares.\n\nEquality is how a program recognises what it was given: the right name, the right answer, the expected command. Since the result is always True or False, an equality comparison is the usual form of the condition that follows if and elif.\n\nThe textbook enters the operators in the interactive shell: 42 == 42 evaluates to True, 42 == 99 evaluates to False, 2 != 3 evaluates to True and 2 != 2 evaluates to False. In programs the typical line compares a variable with a value, as in name == 'Alice' (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nThe operators work with values of any data type (Sweigart, 2025). Strings are compared character by character and capitals count, so 'hello' == 'hello' evaluates to True while 'hello' == 'Hello' evaluates to False. Numbers are compared by their value even when their types differ, so 42 == 42.0 evaluates to True, because Python considers the integer 42 to be the same as the float 42.0 (Sweigart, 2025); the documentation adds that numbers of the built-in numeric types compare mathematically correctly within and across their types (Python Software Foundation, 2026). Values of other, unrelated types are never equal, and no error is raised: the integer 42 does not equal the string '42', so 42 == '42' evaluates to False.\n\nEquality of floating-point numbers needs care. Because floats are stored in binary, the comparison 0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3 evaluates to False, and a program that tests a calculated float for exact equality may never find the answer it expects. The operator != is the exact opposite of ==, so a mistaken == cannot be repaired by reading the result backwards." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_EqualityComparison ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#Equality> .
chd:S_EqualsVersusAssign a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Equality versus assignment"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Equality versus assignment" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 10 ;
    skos:definition "The symbols == and = look alike and do entirely different things. The textbook states the difference in two sentences: the == operator asks whether two values are the same as each other, and the = operator puts the value on the right into the variable on the left (Sweigart, 2025). The first is a comparison and produces a Boolean value; the second is an assignment, which Chapter 1 introduced as a statement that stores a value.\n\nThe two are easy to confuse, and the confusion is expensive: a condition that was meant to ask a question would instead try to store something, and a statement that was meant to store something would only ask a question and discard the answer. A learner who keeps the distinction clear avoids a whole family of errors before they happen.\n\nThe confusion appears in the condition of an if statement, where a beginner writes one equals sign instead of two, and in the line that was meant to be an assignment but was typed as a comparison. The textbook offers a way to remember which is which: the == operator consists of two characters, just like the != operator, and both are comparisons (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nA comparison is an expression, so 4 == 2 + 2 evaluates to True, with the addition done first. An assignment is a statement, so it produces no value to print. Python guards against the most common slip. Writing if x = 3: after x has been assigned raises a SyntaxError with the message invalid syntax. Maybe you meant '==' or ':=' instead of '='?, and the second operator in that message is the assignment expression explained in the branch on expression forms.\n\nThe guard works only where an assignment cannot be valid. The reverse slip, writing == where = was meant, is a legal line that silently does nothing useful, for example spam == 4 on a line by itself, so only careful reading finds it." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_EqualityComparison ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#EqualsVersusAssign> .
chd:S_IdentityComparison a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Identity comparison"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Identity comparison" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 11 ;
    skos:definition "An identity comparison asks whether two expressions refer to one and the same object, not merely to equal values. The operator is evaluates to True for the same object and is not evaluates to True for different objects; the documentation lists them as object identity and negated object identity (Python Software Foundation, 2026). Every object has an identity, and the built-in function id reports it as an integer that is unique and constant for the object during its lifetime (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nIdentity matters for values that exist only once. The reference documentation calls None a singleton, an object of which exactly one exists, and records that the style guide advises comparing singletons with is or is not, never with the equality operators (Python Software Foundation, 2026). The behaviour of is cannot be customised, and it never raises an exception when it is applied to two objects, which makes it the dependable way to ask whether a value is the value None (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nThe everyday use is the test x is None, which asks whether a variable holds the absence of a value. The style guide also asks for the form x is not None instead of not x is None, because the two are equivalent but the first reads better (Van Rossum et al., 2001).\n\nTwo separate objects can be equal without being identical. After a = [1, 2], b = [1, 2] and c = a, the lists a and b have equal values and are different objects, while c is simply a second name for the object a. The program that prints a == b, a is b and a is c therefore prints True False True.\n\nThe operator is is not a stricter ==, and it must not be used to compare numbers or text. Use == to ask whether values are equal and keep is for None, as the style guide advises (Van Rossum et al., 2001). The two operators answer different questions, and a program that mixes them up can work in a test and fail with other data." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_EqualityComparison ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#IdentityComparison> .
chd:S_OrderingComparison a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Ordering comparison"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Ordering comparison" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 12 ;
    skos:definition "Ordering comparisons ask which of two values comes first. The four operators are < (less than), > (greater than), <= (less than or equal to) and >= (greater than or equal to) (Sweigart, 2025). Python also lets several of them be chained in one expression, so that a range can be written the way it is written in mathematics, and it applies the same operators to strings.\n\nPrograms are full of thresholds and limits: an age under twelve, a stock level at least ten, a price not above a budget. Ordering comparisons express each of them in one operator. Because they all return a Boolean value, they join with equality comparisons and with the Boolean operators into conditions of any size.\n\nThe section on ordering shows the four operators on numbers, the section on chained comparison shows how two ordering questions can be asked at once, and the section on string ordering shows that the same operators work on text, by a rule that surprises beginners. The textbook shows the operators on integer and floating-point values (Sweigart, 2025); the reference documentation adds the other cases (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nThe rule that surprises beginners is the one for text. Strings are compared character by character by the Unicode code point of each character, so 'apple' < 'banana' evaluates to True as expected, but 'Zoe' < 'alice' evaluates to True as well, because every capital letter has a smaller code point than every small letter (Python Software Foundation, 2026). A chain is not two comparisons written side by side either: in 12 < len(name) < 20 the middle expression is evaluated once, and the chain stops at the first link that is false." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_Comparison ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#OrderingComparison> .
chd:S_Ordering a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Ordering"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Ordering" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 13 ;
    skos:definition "The ordering operators compare two values by size. The operator < is true when the first value is smaller, > when it is larger, <= when it is smaller or equal, and >= when it is larger or equal (Sweigart, 2025). Each returns True or False, so 42 < 100 evaluates to True and 42 > 100 evaluates to False.\n\nThresholds are everywhere in programs, and every threshold is an ordering comparison: whether someone is old enough for a ticket class, whether a count has reached a limit, whether a result is above zero. The distinction between strict and non-strict forms decides whether the boundary value itself belongs to the range.\n\nThe textbook compares a variable with a value, as in eggs <= 42 and my_age >= 10, and it uses the operator in the conditions of its ticket-style examples, such as age < 12 and age > 100 (Sweigart, 2025). Integers and floats can be mixed freely, so 1.999 < 5 evaluates to True.\n\nThe operators ask a question about order, and the answer depends only on the two values: 42 < 42 evaluates to False because a value is not smaller than itself, while 42 <= 42 evaluates to True. The textbook shows these operators on integer and floating-point values (Sweigart, 2025), and the reference documentation records that they are only defined where they make sense (Python Software Foundation, 2026). Where they do not, Python refuses instead of guessing: 3 < '4' raises a TypeError with the message '<' not supported between instances of 'int' and 'str'.\n\nThe most common mistake is choosing the wrong boundary. The textbook admits that all true programmers have at some point spent an hour finding out that their program does not work because they typed < instead of <= (Sweigart, 2025). When a condition seems to fail only for one exact value, check whether the operator should include its boundary." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_OrderingComparison ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#Ordering> .
chd:S_ChainedComparison a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Chained comparison"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Chained comparison" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 14 ;
    skos:definition "A chained comparison joins several comparison operators in one expression, so that x < y <= z asks both questions at once. The reference documentation defines it as equivalent to x < y and y <= z, except that y is evaluated only once (Python Software Foundation, 2026). In general a op1 b op2 c is equivalent to a op1 b and b op2 c, with each expression evaluated at most once (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nChaining lets a program write a range check the way mathematics writes it. The condition that an age lies from 12 up to but not including 65 becomes 12 <= age < 65 and not the longer 12 <= age and age < 65. The short form is easier to read and cannot be broken by changing the variable name in only one half.\n\nChained comparisons are used in conditions that test whether a value lies in an interval, such as the age bands of a ticket price or a score between two limits. They are a feature of Python that many other languages lack, and the textbook does not introduce them, so a learner meets them first in the documentation and in other people's code.\n\nPython evaluates the chain from left to right and stops as soon as one comparison is false. The expression 1 < 2 < 3 evaluates to True and 3 > 2 > 5 evaluates to False. The stopping rule is visible when a later operand would fail: 5 < 3 < 1/0 evaluates to False without raising a ZeroDivisionError, because the first comparison is already false and the third operand is never evaluated (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nA chain does not compare its outer values with each other: a op1 b op2 c implies no comparison between a and c, so x < y > z is legal, though perhaps not pretty (Python Software Foundation, 2026), and 1 < 2 > 1.5 evaluates to True. Reading a mixed chain as a statement about its two ends is a mistake." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_OrderingComparison ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#ChainedComparison> .
chd:S_StringOrdering a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "String ordering"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "String ordering" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 15 ;
    skos:definition "String ordering is the use of <, >, <= and >= on text. The reference documentation says that strings compare lexicographically, that is, character by character from the left as in a dictionary, using the numerical Unicode code points of their characters (Python Software Foundation, 2026). Unicode is the standard that gives every character a number, called its code point. The code point of a character is its ordinal value, the number that the built-in function ord reports (Python Software Foundation, 2026). The textbook describes the ordering operators for integer and floating-point values only (Sweigart, 2025), so this is a case where the documentation says more than the book.\n\nSorting names, checking whether a word comes before another and arranging records all depend on comparing text. A learner who expects the order of a dictionary will be surprised by the order that Python uses, and the surprise is the source of many bugs, which is the programmers' word for faults in a program, in code that sorts text.\n\nString ordering appears whenever text is placed in order, for example when names are compared, and it explains the order in which sorted text appears. It is also the reason why digits stored as text cannot be compared as numbers.\n\nPython compares the first characters, and only if they are equal does it look at the second ones. The comparison 'apple' < 'banana' evaluates to True because ord('a') is 97 and ord('b') is 98. The capital letters have smaller code points than the lowercase ones, since ord('Z') is 90, so 'Z' < 'a' evaluates to True and 'apple' < 'Apple' evaluates to False.\n\nThe order is that of the code points, not of a human alphabet. The text '10' < '9' evaluates to True because the character '1' comes before '9', although the number 10 is larger than 9, so numbers that arrive as text must be converted before they are compared. The documentation also warns that a character with an accent can be written in more than one way, so that two strings that look identical may compare as different (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_OrderingComparison ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#StringOrdering> .
chd:S_BooleanOperation a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Boolean operation"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Boolean operation" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 16 ;
    skos:definition "Boolean operations combine truth values into new truth values. Python has three Boolean operators, and, or and not. The textbook introduces them as the operators used to compare Boolean values, which, like the comparison operators, evaluate an expression down to a Boolean value (Sweigart, 2025). The reference documentation calls them the Boolean operations and adds a detail that the textbook leaves out: and and or return one of their operands, which is not always a plain True or False (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nA single comparison answers a single question, but real decisions are rarely so simple. A ticket is free when the visitor is under twelve or over sixty-five, and a login succeeds when the name matches and the password matches. Boolean operators build such compound conditions out of simple ones, and without them every combination of facts would need a decision of its own nested inside another.\n\nBoolean operators appear in the condition of an if or elif statement, usually joining comparisons. The second short program of the chapter uses or so that the unit of a hard drive is recognised whether the user types it in uppercase or in lowercase (Sweigart, 2025). Anywhere that a program wants to say 'this and that', 'this or that' or 'not this', it uses one of the three.\n\nThe branch has two parts. The logical operators are explained one at a time, together with the number of operands each takes and the truth table that lists every possible result. The evaluation section then explains how Python works through a compound expression: from left to right, stopping as soon as the answer is known, returning one of the operands, mixing in comparisons, and giving not priority over and and and priority over or.\n\nThe rule that Boolean operators always produce True or False is correct only when their operands are themselves True or False. With other values, and and or hand back one of the operands, and the documentation names this as an important exception to the rule that operations with a Boolean result return True or False (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#BooleanOperation> .
chd:S_LogicalOperator a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Logical operator"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Logical operator" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 17 ;
    skos:definition "The logical operators are and, or and not. The first two join two truth values, and the third reverses one. Together they cover the three basic ways of reasoning about yes and no: requiring that both facts hold, requiring that at least one holds, and asking that a fact does not hold (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nEvery compound condition in a program is built from these three words, so a precise knowledge of what each does is the foundation for reading and writing conditions. Their meanings are fixed by tables of results, which makes them easy to check completely.\n\nThe branch explains each operator in turn, with the examples of the textbook, and then two ideas that describe them all: the number of operands an operator takes, which separates not from and and or, and the truth table, a complete list of the results an operator can give.\n\nThe tables are small enough to hold in full, which is the point of them. Each of and and or has four rows, one for every pairing of two truth values, and not has two, so ten rows describe the three operators completely (Sweigart, 2025). The count is also why their order matters: not binds more tightly than the other two, so not True and False evaluates to False, which is the reading (not True) and False, while not (True and False) evaluates to True instead." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_BooleanOperation ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#LogicalOperator> .
chd:S_AndOperator a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "And operator"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "And operator" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 18 ;
    skos:definition "The and operator joins two Boolean values or expressions and evaluates to True only if both of them are True; otherwise it evaluates to False (Sweigart, 2025). Because it always takes two operands, the textbook calls it a binary Boolean operator.\n\nThe operator expresses a requirement that all of several conditions hold at once: the name is correct and the password is correct, the visitor is an adult and has a ticket. A single failing part is enough to make the whole condition false, which is exactly the behaviour that a security check or an eligibility rule needs.\n\nThe textbook enters True and True, which evaluates to True, and True and False, which evaluates to False. It also joins comparisons, so (4 < 5) and (5 < 6) evaluates to True while (4 < 5) and (9 < 6) evaluates to False, because the second comparison fails (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nPython evaluates the left operand first. According to the reference documentation, x and y first evaluates x; if x is false, its value is returned, and otherwise y is evaluated and its value is returned (Python Software Foundation, 2026). For Boolean operands that rule gives the same answers as the table of results: the four combinations True and True, True and False, False and True and False and False evaluate to True, False, False and False in that order.\n\nEach side of and must be a complete expression. A programmer who has a range in mind may write age > 12 and < 65, which Python rejects as a SyntaxError with the message invalid syntax; the correct line repeats the variable, as in age > 12 and age < 65, or uses a chained comparison. Remember also that and can return an operand instead of a Boolean value, as the section on operand results shows." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_LogicalOperator ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#AndOperator> .
chd:S_OrOperator a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Or operator"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Or operator" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 19 ;
    skos:definition "The or operator joins two Boolean values or expressions and evaluates to True if either of them is True. Only when both are False does it evaluate to False (Sweigart, 2025). Like and, it takes two operands and is therefore a binary Boolean operator.\n\nThe operator expresses alternatives: any one of several conditions is enough. It lets a program accept several spellings of the same answer, or apply one rule to several groups, without repeating the action for each of them.\n\nThe textbook enters False or True, which evaluates to True, and False or False, which evaluates to False, and it combines comparisons, so (1 == 2) or (2 == 2) evaluates to True. The second short program of the chapter uses the test unit == 'TB' or unit == 'tb' so that the program works whether the user enters the unit in uppercase or in lowercase (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nPython evaluates the left operand first. The reference documentation states that x or y first evaluates x; if x is true, its value is returned, and otherwise y is evaluated and its value is returned (Python Software Foundation, 2026). For Boolean operands this is the same as the table of results: only the combination False or False evaluates to False.\n\nA classic mistake is to write the second comparison in a shortened form, such as unit == 'TB' or 'tb'. The right-hand operand 'tb' is a non-empty string, which counts as true, so the whole condition is true whatever the unit is: with the unit 'GB' the expression evaluates to 'tb', which an if statement reads as true. Each alternative needs its own complete comparison." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_LogicalOperator ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#OrOperator> .
chd:S_NotOperator a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Not operator"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Not operator" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 20 ;
    skos:definition "The not operator reverses a truth value. Unlike and and or, it operates on only one Boolean value or expression, which makes it a unary operator, and it evaluates to the opposite Boolean value (Sweigart, 2025). The expression not True evaluates to False, and not False evaluates to True.\n\nMany conditions are most naturally stated as a negation: the file is not found, the list is not empty, the answer is not yes. The operator also reverses a stored flag in a single step, which is how the Opposite Day program of the textbook flips its variable.\n\nThe textbook shows that not can be repeated, in the way a double negative can in speech, so not not not not True evaluates to True, and adds that there is never a real reason to do this in programs (Sweigart, 2025). In real code the operator usually appears in front of a comparison or a variable that holds a Boolean value.\n\nThe reference documentation defines it by cases: the operator not yields True if its argument is false and False otherwise (Python Software Foundation, 2026). Because not has to create a new value, it always returns a Boolean value, whatever the type of its argument: not 'foo' produces False and not '' produces True. It also has a lower priority than the comparison operators, so not 4 == 5 is read as not (4 == 5) and evaluates to True (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nThe operator belongs in front of an expression, never inside a comparison. Writing 4 == not 5 is a SyntaxError with the message invalid syntax, and the documentation states that a == not b is a syntax error (Python Software Foundation, 2026). When a negation is hard to read, a parenthesis or a reversed comparison, such as != instead of not ... ==, is clearer." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_LogicalOperator ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#NotOperator> .
chd:S_OperatorArity a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Binary and unary operators"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Binary and unary operators" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 21 ;
    skos:definition "The arity of an operator is the number of operands that it takes. A binary operator takes two, one on each side, and a unary operator takes one. In the Boolean operators, and and or are binary, whereas not is unary (Sweigart, 2025). The reference documentation states the general convention that, unless the syntax is explicitly given, the operators in its precedence table are binary (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nKnowing how many operands an operator wants explains many error messages and many reading mistakes. An operator with a missing operand cannot be evaluated, and an operator that applies to one operand binds to whatever follows it, which decides what a negation reverses.\n\nA symbol can serve in both roles. The minus sign is binary in 5 - 3, which subtracts, and unary in -(2 + 3), which the documentation describes as yielding the negation of its numeric argument, so the expression evaluates to -5 (Python Software Foundation, 2026). Chapter 1 met the same sign in -3 ** 2, where it is unary.\n\nA binary operator can be repeated to join many operands, because each use takes the result of the one before it: a and b and c is read as (a and b) and c. A unary operator applies to the single expression on its right. This is why not 2 + 2 == 5 reverses the whole comparison and not only the number 2: the unary not takes everything that binds more tightly than itself.\n\nDo not read a unary operator as a binary one. The expression 4 - -2 contains a binary minus sign followed by a unary one and evaluates to 6, which many readers do not expect at first sight. When a line mixes the two uses, parentheses show the reader which is which, as in 4 - (-2)." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_LogicalOperator ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#OperatorArity> .
chd:S_TruthTable a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Truth table"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Truth table" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 22 ;
    skos:definition "A truth table shows every possible result of a Boolean operator (Sweigart, 2025). Each row lists one combination of values for the operands and the value to which the operator evaluates for that combination. Because a Boolean value has only two possibilities, the number of rows is small and the table is complete.\n\nThe table is a full definition of an operator, not an example of it. Nothing about and, or or not remains to be guessed once the table is known, and a learner can check any reading of a compound condition by writing out the rows. The practice questions at the end of the chapter ask the reader to write the tables from memory for exactly this reason (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nThe textbook gives three tables, Table 2-2 for and, Table 2-3 for or and Table 2-4 for not (Sweigart, 2025). They are met again in the practice questions at the end of the chapter, and a learner can rebuild them at any time by evaluating the expressions in the interactive shell.\n\nAn operator with two Boolean operands has four rows, because each operand can take two values. For and the four rows evaluate to True, False, False and False, for the operands in the order True and True, True and False, False and True, False and False. For or the same rows evaluate to True, True, True and False. The operator not takes one operand, so its table has two rows: not True evaluates to False and not False evaluates to True.\n\nA truth table describes the behaviour of the operators on True and False only. With other kinds of value, and and or return an operand, as the section on operand results explains, and a table written for Boolean values then no longer lists the results. The tables also say nothing about the order in which operands are evaluated, which matters when an operand has an effect of its own." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_LogicalOperator ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#TruthTable> .
chd:S_Evaluation a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Evaluation"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Evaluation" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 23 ;
    skos:definition "Evaluation is the process by which Python works out the value of an expression (Python Software Foundation, 2026). For compound Boolean expressions it follows four rules that this branch explains: the operands are evaluated from left to right and evaluation stops as soon as the answer is known, the result can be one of the operands, comparisons and Boolean operators can be mixed freely, and the three Boolean operators have a fixed order of priority.\n\nTwo readers who follow the same rules reach the same answer, and a program that follows them behaves the way its author intended. The rules also give the operators practical powers beyond the truth tables: a condition can protect a risky calculation, and a default value can replace an empty one.\n\nThe sections of this branch go from the mechanism to the rules that govern its use. Short-circuit evaluation and operand results describe what the operators do with their operands, the section on Boolean expressions shows comparisons and Boolean operators together, and the final section gives the order in which not, and and or are applied.\n\nThe rules show themselves in what an expression returns rather than in what it decides. The expression 0 or 'default' evaluates to 'default' and '' or 0 evaluates to 0, because or hands back the operand it stopped at and not a Boolean (Python Software Foundation, 2026). Stopping early is what lets a condition protect a risky calculation: with n bound to 0 the expression n != 0 and 100 / n evaluates to False and the division never runs, whereas the same two operands in the other order raise ZeroDivisionError." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_BooleanOperation ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#Evaluation> .
chd:S_ShortCircuit a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Short circuit"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Short circuit" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 24 ;
    skos:definition "Short-circuit evaluation means that the operators and and or evaluate their second operand only when the first operand has not already decided the result. The documentation states it in two notes: or only evaluates the second argument if the first one is false, and and only evaluates the second argument if the first one is true (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nThe rule is a safeguard and a saving. A condition can be arranged so that the first operand checks that the second operand is safe to evaluate, and an expensive or risky second operand is skipped whenever the first one settles the answer. Programmers use this to write checks in a single line that would otherwise need a nested decision.\n\nThe pattern is found in conditions that guard a division or a lookup, as in x != 0 and 10 / x > 2, and in conditions that supply a fallback. The textbook does not introduce short-circuit evaluation, but its operators behave this way in every program it shows (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nPython evaluates the first operand and looks at its truth. For and, a false first operand settles the result, so the second is not evaluated; for or, a true first operand settles it. The expression 1 or 1 / 0 evaluates to 1 and the expression 0 and 1 / 0 evaluates to 0, and in neither case is the division carried out. When the second operand is needed it is evaluated, so 1 and 1 / 0 raises a ZeroDivisionError. With x = 0, the condition x != 0 and 10 / x > 2 evaluates to False without any error.\n\nAnything that the skipped operand would have done is skipped too. If the second operand is a call to a function that prints, the printing does not happen when the first operand settles the result: False and print('skipped') prints nothing, while True and print('shown') prints shown. A programmer must not rely on a skipped operand to run, and should not hide needed actions inside a condition." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_Evaluation ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#ShortCircuit> .
chd:S_OperandResult a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Operand result"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Operand result" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 25 ;
    skos:definition "The result of and and or is one of their operands. The reference documentation says that neither operator restricts the value and type it returns to False and True, but rather returns the last evaluated argument (Python Software Foundation, 2026). The rule is that x and y returns x if x is false and y otherwise, and that x or y returns x if x is true and y otherwise.\n\nThis behaviour is deliberate and useful. The documentation gives the example of a string that should be replaced by a default value if it is empty: the expression s or 'foo' yields the desired value (Python Software Foundation, 2026). A single operator therefore does the work of a small decision, which is why the idiom is so common in Python code.\n\nThe idiom appears wherever an empty or missing value should be replaced by a fallback, for example a name that the user left blank, and in chains of alternatives where the first usable value wins. The operator not is the exception, because it has to create a new value and therefore always returns True or False (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nThe returned operand is the one that settled the result. The expression 'hi' and 'there' evaluates to 'there', because the first operand is true and the second is the last one evaluated, and '' and 'there' evaluates to '', the first operand, which is already false. In the same way 0 or 'default' evaluates to 'default' and 'set' or 'default' evaluates to 'set'. A chain returns the operand that decides it: 1 and 2 and 3 evaluates to 3 and 0 or [] or None evaluates to None.\n\nWhen the value is printed or stored, it is the operand and not a Boolean value that is seen. If a strict True or False is needed, the function bool converts the result, as in bool('hi' and 'there'). The surprise is greatest when a condition is shown to a user, because a text such as 'there' appears where the reader expected True." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_Evaluation ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#OperandResult> .
chd:S_BooleanExpression a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Boolean expression"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Boolean expression" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 26 ;
    skos:definition "A Boolean expression is an expression whose value is True or False, built from comparisons and Boolean operators. The textbook explains that an expression such as 4 < 5 is not itself a Boolean value, but it is an expression that evaluates down to one, so comparison operators can be used inside expressions with the Boolean operators (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nReal conditions mix both kinds of operator: a value is compared with a limit, and several such comparisons are then joined by and, or and not. Without the ability to mix them, each comparison would have to be stored in a variable before it could be combined.\n\nThe textbook enters (4 < 5) and (5 < 6), which evaluates to True, (4 < 5) and (9 < 6), which evaluates to False, and (1 == 2) or (2 == 2), which evaluates to True. The conditions of the programs in the chapter, such as unit == 'TB' or unit == 'tb', have the same form (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nThe computer evaluates the left expression first and then the right expression. When it knows the Boolean value of each, it evaluates the whole expression down to one Boolean value (Sweigart, 2025). A longer line follows the same path. With spam = 4, the expression 2 + 2 == spam and not 2 + 2 == (spam + 1) and 2 * 2 == 2 + 2 evaluates to True: the arithmetic comes first, then the comparisons, then not, then and.\n\nParentheses around the comparisons are not required, but they make a line easier to read, and a long Boolean expression is easier to check when it is split into named parts. A common slip is to forget that the operators work on whole expressions: spam == 3 or 4 does not ask whether spam is 3 or 4, since the 4 on the right is a value that counts as true." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_Evaluation ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#BooleanExpression> .
chd:S_BooleanPrecedence a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Boolean precedence"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Boolean precedence" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 27 ;
    skos:definition "Boolean precedence is the order in which a Python expression applies not, and and or when it contains several of them. The Boolean operators have an order of operations just like the math operators do: after any math and comparison operators evaluate, Python evaluates the not operators first, then the and operators, and then the or operators (Sweigart, 2025). The reference documentation lists the same order from the lowest priority to the highest as or, and, not (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nPrecedence makes an expression mean exactly one thing. Without it, a line that mixes and with or could be read as joining its parts in either order, and the two readings can give different answers. Chapter 1 introduced the idea for arithmetic; this section extends it to truth values.\n\nEvery compound condition that mixes the three operators depends on it, and so does every condition that combines comparisons with them, because comparisons are evaluated before the Boolean operators.\n\nIn True or False and False the operator and is applied first, giving False, and then or gives True, so the expression evaluates to True. Parentheses change the order: (True or False) and False evaluates to False. The operator not binds more tightly than and, so not True and False is read as (not True) and False and evaluates to False, whereas not (True and False) evaluates to True.\n\nDo not rely on memory for a line that matters. A reader who has forgotten the order can read the line wrongly, and a parenthesis costs nothing. The common error is to expect that and and or are applied from left to right with equal weight, which would give a different answer for the first example above." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_Evaluation ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#BooleanPrecedence> .
chd:S_FlowControl a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Flow control"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Flow control" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 28 ;
    skos:definition "Flow control is the part of a program that decides which instructions run, in what order and how often. The textbook puts it plainly: based on how expressions evaluate, a program can decide to skip instructions, repeat them, or choose one of several instructions to run, and flow control statements decide which Python instructions to execute under which conditions (Sweigart, 2025). This chapter treats the first of these abilities, choosing; repeating instructions, which is done with loops (statements that run a block again and again), is the subject of the next chapter.\n\nA program that ran every line from the first to the last, in order, every time, could never react to its input. The textbook observes that you almost never want your programs to start from the first line of code and simply execute every line, straight to the end (Sweigart, 2025). Flow control is what turns a list of instructions into a program that responds to its data.\n\nFlow control appears in every program of more than a few lines. Checking a password, sorting out which message to show, refusing invalid input and handling the several cases of a command all use the statements of this branch. The textbook illustrates them with flowcharts, in which the branching points are drawn as diamonds, so that the statement and the picture can be compared (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nA flow control statement usually has a condition, which is an expression that Python tests for truth, and a block of indented lines that it runs or skips. The branch therefore first explains how statements are executed and how a flowchart shows the path of execution. It then explains the parts of a flow control statement, namely the condition, the block, the indentation that marks the block, the clause and the keyword that opens it. After that come the if, elif and else statements and the chain they form, and last the two expression forms that bring a choice or a name into a single expression.\n\nThe central mistake in this branch is to confuse a line that is written with a line that is executed. A line inside a block that is skipped never runs, so an error in it is not seen, and a line that is outdented by one level may run when the author thought it was skipped. Reading a program means following the path that its data takes through the conditions." ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#FlowControl> .
chd:S_ExecutionFlow a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Execution flow"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Execution flow" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 29 ;
    skos:definition "Execution flow is the path that execution takes through a program: which statement runs first, which runs next and which are skipped. Three ideas belong to it. A statement is the unit that is executed, program execution is the name for the instruction that is being executed at a given moment, and a flowchart is a diagram that draws the possible paths (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nThese three ideas give a learner the vocabulary for reasoning about a program before running it. Predicting what a program will print means following its execution, and explaining what went wrong means finding the point at which execution took a different path from the one that was intended.\n\nThe branch starts with the statement, the building block that the rest of the chapter specialises into flow control statements. It continues with program execution, which the textbook explains with the picture of a finger moving down the screen, and it ends with the flowchart, which turns that finger's path into a drawing.\n\nThe vocabulary pays for itself the first time a program appears to do nothing. A statement is executed for its effect, so a line that reads spam == 42 on its own is a complete and legal program that computes a Boolean and throws it away, while spam = 42 binds a name. Python also compiles a whole file before running any of it, which is why a syntax error on the last line stops the first line from printing at all (Python Software Foundation, 2026). Only after that does execution follow the path a flowchart draws, one statement at a time." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_FlowControl ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#ExecutionFlow> .
chd:S_FlowControlStatement a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Flow control statement"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Flow control statement" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 30 ;
    skos:definition "A statement is the unit of a program that Python executes. The glossary defines it as part of a suite, a block of code, and as either an expression or one of several constructs with a keyword, such as if, while or for (Python Software Foundation, 2026). A flow control statement is a statement of the second kind: it does not compute a value for use elsewhere, but decides which other statements run (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nThe distinction between an expression and a statement explains what a line can be used for. An expression has a value and can be printed, stored or combined with others. A statement does something, and the glossary notes that some constructs are statements which cannot be used as expressions: it names while as an example and adds that assignments are statements, not expressions (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nThe if statement, with its elif and else parts, is the flow control statement of this chapter, and the next chapter adds the loop statements while and for. All of them are compound statements, which the reference documentation describes as statements that contain groups of other statements and affect or control how those statements are executed (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nA compound statement consists of one or more clauses, and each clause consists of a header and a suite, which is the group of statements that the clause controls (Python Software Foundation, 2026). The headers of one compound statement stand at the same indentation level, and the textbook says the same in its own words: flow control statements often start with a condition and are always followed by a block of code (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nA statement produces no value to print or store. Python makes the boundary visible: an assignment such as spam = 42 does not show a result in the interactive shell, whereas the expression spam does. When a decision has to produce a value inside a larger expression, the statement is the wrong tool and the conditional expression described later in this chapter is the right one." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_ExecutionFlow ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#FlowControlStatement> .
chd:S_ProgramExecution a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Program execution"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Program execution" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 31 ;
    skos:definition "Program execution, or simply execution, is a term for the current instruction being executed (Sweigart, 2025). It is not a property of the text of a program but of a particular run: the same program can follow different paths on different occasions. The interpreter of Chapter 1 is the program that carries out the execution.\n\nThe idea separates what is written from what happens. A reader who can say which line is being executed, and why the next one follows, can find a bug without running anything. The textbook offers a physical picture for it: if you put your finger on each line on your screen as the line is executed, you can think of your finger as the program execution (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nIn the hello.py program of Chapter 1, Python started executing instructions at the top and went down, one after another (Sweigart, 2025). Every program in this chapter starts that way too, but as soon as it contains a flow control statement the finger may jump over a block or choose between two.\n\nWithout flow control, execution moves straight down the program. With flow control statements, the textbook says, the finger will jump to different places in the source code based on conditions (Sweigart, 2025). In a program that sets age = 70 and then has an if statement that prints a message only when age > 65 holds, the line inside the block runs for this age, and it would be skipped for the age 20.\n\nA line that is never executed is never checked for the errors that appear while a program runs. A program that contains if False: followed by a block that adds a number to a string, and then a print of the word done, runs without any error, because the faulty line is not reached. A syntax error is different: the parser finds it when it reads the program text, so a mistake in the shape of a skipped line is still reported, and nothing of the program runs." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_ExecutionFlow ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#ProgramExecution> .
chd:S_Flowchart a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Flowchart"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Flowchart" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 32 ;
    skos:definition "A flowchart is a diagram of the steps of a process and the decisions between them. The textbook states that flow control statements directly correspond to the symbols in a flowchart, and it draws a flowchart for every statement that it introduces (Sweigart, 2025). Branching points are drawn as diamonds, the other steps as rectangles, and the starting and ending steps as rounded rectangles.\n\nA flowchart shows all paths at once, which the text of a program does not. In a flowchart there is usually more than one way to go from the start to the end, and the same is true for the lines of a program (Sweigart, 2025). Drawing the paths is a way to plan a decision before writing it, and to check afterwards that every path leads somewhere sensible.\n\nThe chapter opens with a flowchart of what to do if it is raining, which a reader follows along the arrows from Start to End. Figures 2-2 to 2-7 then give a flowchart for the if statement, the else statement, the elif statement and the programs vampire.py, vampire2.py and littleKid.py (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nEach diamond stands for a condition, and the arrows leaving it lead to the steps that follow when the condition is true or false. Following the arrows from the start to the end reproduces the path that execution takes. Once the statements are written as code, the diamonds become the if and elif statements and the rectangles become their blocks.\n\nA flowchart can reveal a bug that the code hides. The flowchart of the program vampire2.py in the textbook has a path that will logically never happen, because a person older than 2000 is already older than 100 and the first of those two tests catches them; the figure caption says so (Sweigart, 2025). A branch that can never be taken is a signal that the conditions are in the wrong order." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_ExecutionFlow ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#Flowchart> .
chd:S_ControlStructure a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Control structure"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Control structure" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 33 ;
    skos:definition "A control structure is the set of parts out of which a flow control statement is built. A statement of this kind starts with a keyword and a condition, ends its first line with a colon, and is followed by a block of indented lines, which is called its clause (Sweigart, 2025). This branch names those parts: the condition, the block, the indentation that marks the block, the clause and the keyword.\n\nLearning the parts first makes every statement that follows easy to read. If, elif and else differ in the keyword and in whether they have a condition, but they share the shape of header, colon and indented block, and the same shape returns in the loops and function definitions of later chapters.\n\nThe five sections of the branch follow the order in which a reader meets the parts on the page: the condition that Python tests, the block that it controls, the indentation that Python reads to find the block, the clause that is the header and block together, and the keyword that opens the clause.\n\nBecause Python reads the indentation, the layout is part of the grammar and not a matter of taste. A line indented more deeply than the block it joins is refused with IndentationError: unexpected indent, and a tab mixed with spaces inside one block raises TabError: inconsistent use of tabs and spaces in indentation, since the interpreter cannot then tell which lines belong together (Python Software Foundation, 2026). The style guide settles the amount at four spaces for each level, which is how every block in this chapter is written (Van Rossum et al., 2001)." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_FlowControl ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#ControlStructure> .
chd:S_Condition a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Condition"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Condition" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 34 ;
    skos:definition "A condition is an expression that a flow control statement evaluates to decide what to do. The textbook explains that the Boolean expressions seen so far could all be considered conditions, which are the same thing as expressions; condition is just a more specific name in the context of flow control statements (Sweigart, 2025). An if statement, for example, executes its block when its condition is true and skips it when its condition is false.\n\nAlmost every flow control statement uses a condition, so a condition is how a program connects its data to its decisions (Sweigart, 2025). The textbook gives two ways to say it in English: if this condition is true, do this thing, or else do this other thing; and keep repeating these instructions as long as this condition continues to be true.\n\nA condition follows the word if or elif, as in name == 'Alice' or age < 12. The textbook also uses conditions that combine several comparisons, such as unit == 'TB' or unit == 'tb'. The second form of the English sentence, repeating while a condition holds, belongs to the loop statements of the next chapter.\n\nPython evaluates the condition and reads the result as true or false. The reference documentation says that the if statement selects exactly one of its suites by evaluating the expressions one by one until one is found to be true (Python Software Foundation, 2026). The textbook says that a condition always evaluates to a Boolean value, and that is the usual case, but Python accepts any value and applies the rule of truthiness, so a line such as if name: is legal.\n\nA condition is an expression, not a statement. An assignment cannot take its place, and the slip of writing one equals sign instead of two in the condition of an if statement is reported as a syntax error. Spelling the condition out more than necessary is also a mistake of style: the style guide advises against comparing a Boolean value with True or False using ==, so if greeting: is preferred to if greeting == True: (Van Rossum et al., 2001)." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_ControlStructure ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#Condition> .
chd:S_Block a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Block"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Block" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 35 ;
    skos:definition "A block is a group of lines of code that are treated together. The textbook explains that lines of Python code can be grouped together in blocks, and that you can tell when a block begins and ends from the indentation of the lines of code (Sweigart, 2025). The reference documentation calls such a group a suite, a group of statements controlled by a clause (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nA block decides what belongs to a decision. Everything that is part of the block runs together when the condition holds, and everything that is outside it runs regardless. Because Python marks blocks by indentation, the layout that a human reads and the structure that the interpreter reads are one and the same, so a program cannot look as if it did one thing while doing another.\n\nA block follows every flow control statement of this chapter: the lines after if, after elif and after else. The textbook points out that blocks can be found inside other blocks, and that this chapter uses blocks only with if, elif and else, although several other statements use blocks as well (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nThe textbook gives four rules for blocks. A new block begins when the indentation increases. Blocks can contain other blocks. A block ends when the indentation decreases to zero or to a containing block's indentation. And Python expects a new block immediately after any statement that ends with a colon (Sweigart, 2025). In its example the first block is the line print('Hello, Mary'), which contains the lines after it, including an inner block with the single line print('Access granted.') and another with print('Wrong password.').\n\nOne level of indentation decides whether a line is part of a block. In a program that prints big inside the block of if x > 3: and then prints always, the second line is part of the block when it is indented and runs every time when it is not. An error in the indentation does not look like an error to a reader, so the structure of the blocks is the first thing to check when a program behaves strangely." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_ControlStructure ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#Block> .
chd:S_Indentation a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Indentation"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Indentation" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 36 ;
    skos:definition "Indentation is the whitespace at the beginning of a line of code. The language reference says that leading whitespace at the beginning of a logical line (one statement, even when it is written over several physical lines) is used to compute the indentation level of the line, which in turn is used to determine the grouping of statements (Python Software Foundation, 2026). In Python it is part of the syntax, not decoration.\n\nMany languages mark blocks with braces and treat the layout as a matter of taste. Python lets the layout carry the meaning, so that a program that looks right is right about its structure. The textbook adds the sentence that Python uses indentation to create blocks of code, and the language reference gives the exact procedure (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nEvery flow control statement is followed by indented lines, and a statement nested inside a block (placed inside it, as a part of it) is indented one level further, as in the textbook's example of an if statement inside another if statement (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nTo read the indentation, Python splits the text of a program into tokens, the smallest units of the text such as names, numbers, operators and line ends. It keeps a stack of indentation levels, a list in which only the last entry is looked at and changed, starting with a single zero. At the beginning of each logical line it compares the indentation of the line with the top of the stack. If the two are equal, nothing happens. If the line is indented more, the new level is pushed and an INDENT token is produced, which opens a block. If the line is indented less, the level must be one that is on the stack, and each larger level that is removed produces a DEDENT token, which closes a block (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nWrong indentation is reported as an IndentationError, a kind of SyntaxError. A block that is missing after a colon gives expected an indented block after 'if' statement on line 1; a line that is indented for no reason gives unexpected indent; and a line that returns to a level that does not exist gives unindent does not match any outer indentation level. Mixing tabs and spaces in the same file can also cause an error, and a TabError is raised when the meaning would depend on the width of a tab (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_ControlStructure ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#Indentation> .
chd:S_Clause a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Clause"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Clause" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 37 ;
    skos:definition "A clause is one part of a compound statement. In the textbook's wording, flow control statements are always followed by a block of code called the clause; the block after an if statement is the if clause, and the block after else is the else clause (Sweigart, 2025). The reference documentation uses the word for the header and its suite together: a compound statement consists of one or more clauses, and a clause consists of a header and a suite (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nThe word lets a learner talk about a single branch of an if chain without mixing it up with the whole statement. Two usages exist, and both are common: the textbook calls the controlled block the clause, whereas the reference calls the header with its block a clause. Knowing both prevents misunderstanding when one source is read after the other.\n\nAn if statement has an if clause, possibly followed by elif clauses and an else clause. In the reference documentation each clause header begins with a uniquely identifying keyword and ends with a colon, and the headers of one compound statement stand at the same indentation level (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nThe suite of a clause can be written in two ways: as one or more indented statements on the following lines, which is the form the textbook teaches, or as simple statements on the same line as the header. Only the indented form can contain nested compound statements (Python Software Foundation, 2026). The style guide discourages putting the suite on the header's line, and it says never to do so for clauses of multi-clause statements (Van Rossum et al., 2001).\n\nThe colon at the end of the header is required. Without it Python reports a SyntaxError with the message expected ':' for a line such as if True. After the colon the next line must be indented, or the IndentationError described under indentation is raised." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_ControlStructure ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#Clause> .
chd:S_Keyword a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Keyword"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Keyword" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 38 ;
    skos:definition "A keyword is a word that the language reserves for its own use. The language reference states that keywords cannot be used as ordinary identifiers, and that they must be spelled exactly as written; its list includes False, None, True, and, or, not, if, elif and else (Python Software Foundation, 2026). They are what a reader sees at the start of a flow control statement.\n\nKeywords tell the interpreter what kind of statement a line is. The word if starts a decision and the word else starts its alternative. Because the words are reserved, a program cannot give them a second meaning: the assignment of a value to True is illegal for this reason.\n\nThe if, elif and else of this chapter are keywords, and so are True, False, None, and, or and not. The standard library offers a module named keyword that can test a word: keyword.iskeyword('if') evaluates to True, and keyword.iskeyword('true') evaluates to False because the lowercase spelling is not reserved (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nPython recognises keywords when it reads the program text. Every keyword is written in lowercase except False, None and True, which begin with a capital letter. A few words are reserved only in some contexts; these soft keywords are explained in the section on the match statement, where match and case are the examples.\n\nA keyword cannot be used as the name of a variable. The line if = 3 is rejected with a SyntaxError whose message is invalid syntax. Beginners meet this when they choose natural names, and the remedy is to pick another name. A word that merely looks like a keyword, such as the lowercase true, is not reserved and is reported as an unknown name instead." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_ControlStructure ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#Keyword> .
chd:S_Branching a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Branching"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Branching" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 39 ;
    skos:definition "Branching is the choice between alternative blocks of code. In Python it is done with the if statement and with the elif and else clauses that may follow it. The textbook describes the most common flow control statement as the if statement and the others as additions to it (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nBranching is what makes a program respond to its input. Each additional clause adds an alternative, and the reader of a program can see the alternatives one below the other. The tutorial notes that an if, elif and else sequence is a substitute for the switch or case statements found in other languages (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nThe branch takes the if statement first, then the elif clause that adds a further condition, then the else clause that handles everything that is left, and finally the chain that the three form together and the rules for putting them in order.\n\nExactly one clause of a chain runs, and it is the first whose condition is true. In a chain that tests age < 12 and then age < 20, an age of 10 takes the first clause and the second is never reached, so exchanging the two conditions changes the answer for every age under twelve (Sweigart, 2025). A chain with no else clause may run none of its blocks at all, which is the usual cause of the unassigned name that the decision patterns of this chapter meet later." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_FlowControl ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#Branching> .
chd:S_IfStatement a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "If statement"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "If statement" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 40 ;
    skos:definition "The if statement is the most common flow control statement. Its block, the if clause, executes when the condition of the statement is True, and the block is skipped when the condition is False (Sweigart, 2025). In Python an if statement consists of the if keyword, a condition, a colon and, starting on the next line, an indented block of code (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nThe statement lets a program do something only in the cases in which it is meant. Checking whether a person's name is Alice, whether a number is large enough, or whether a file was found are each one if statement. All other branching builds on it, since elif and else clauses are additions to an if.\n\nThe textbook's example checks a name: with name = 'Alice' and the statement if name == 'Alice': followed by an indented print('Hi, Alice.'), the program prints Hi, Alice. When the variable holds another string, the message does not appear because the block is skipped (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nPython evaluates the condition and reads its value as true or false. If it is true, the indented block runs and execution then continues with the first line after the block; if it is false, the block is skipped. The reference documentation puts it as selecting exactly one of the suites by evaluating the expressions one by one until one is found to be true, after which no other part of the statement is executed or evaluated (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nThe three details that cause most errors are the colon after the condition, the indentation of the block and the comparison operator in the condition. A missing colon is reported as a SyntaxError, a missing block as an IndentationError, and a single equals sign in the condition as a SyntaxError that suggests ==. The block is skipped without any message when the condition is false, so a program that prints nothing is not necessarily broken." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_Branching ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#IfStatement> .
chd:S_ElifClause a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Elif clause"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Elif clause" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 41 ;
    skos:definition "The elif clause is an else-if clause. It always follows an if statement or another elif clause, and it provides another condition that is checked only if all of the previous conditions were False (Sweigart, 2025). Like the if statement it consists of a keyword, a condition, a colon and an indented block of code (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nUse if or else when only one of two clauses should run. Use elif when one of many possible clauses should run, as when several age groups each get a different message. The tutorial says that the keyword elif is short for else if and is useful to avoid excessive indentation (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nThe textbook adds an elif to its name checker so that a person younger than twelve receives another greeting, and then adds two more for ages above 2000 and above 100. With name = 'Carol' and age = 3000 the chain prints the sentence Unlike you, Alice is not an undead, immortal vampire (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nPython tests the conditions from top to bottom. The elif clause runs if its condition is True and every earlier condition was False. Once one condition is found to be True, the rest of the elif clauses are automatically skipped, and when all conditions are False Python skips all of the clauses (Sweigart, 2025). In a chain of elif statements only one or none of the clauses will be executed.\n\nThe order of the elif clauses matters. If the test age > 100 is written before the test age > 2000, a person aged 3000 satisfies the first one and the second can never be reached: the program prints You are not Alice, grannie. The textbook calls this a bug that is introduced by rearranging the clauses (Sweigart, 2025). Put the more specific condition before the more general one." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_Branching ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#ElifClause> .
chd:S_ElseClause a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Else clause"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Else clause" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 42 ;
    skos:definition "The else clause is the part of an if statement that runs when nothing else has. An if clause can optionally be followed by an else statement, and the else clause is executed only when the condition of the if statement is False (Sweigart, 2025). Unlike if and elif, an else statement has no condition: it consists of the keyword else, a colon and an indented block.\n\nThe else clause makes sure that every case is handled. Without it a chain of conditions can end without doing anything, and the program carries on as if a decision had been made. With it the programmer states what happens in all other cases, which is often an error message.\n\nIn the textbook's name checker, the else clause offers a different greeting if the person's name is not Alice: the program prints Hello, stranger. For a person named Carol aged 3000, with an if, an elif for ages below twelve and an else clause, it prints the sentence You are neither Alice nor a little kid (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nThe reference documentation says that if all expressions are false, the suite of the else clause, if present, is executed (Python Software Foundation, 2026). The textbook draws the consequence for chains: with an else after the last elif, it is guaranteed that at least one, and only one, of the clauses will be executed (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nThe else clause must come last. Python 3.14 reports an elif that follows an else with the message 'elif' block follows an 'else' block, whereas Python 3.12 says only invalid syntax. An else clause has no condition of its own, so it cannot test anything: a line such as else x > 3: is a SyntaxError with the message invalid syntax." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_Branching ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#ElseClause> .
chd:S_BranchChain a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Branch chain"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Branch chain" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 43 ;
    skos:definition "A branch chain is an if statement together with the elif clauses and the else clause that follow it. The textbook states two rules for putting them together: there is always exactly one if statement, with any elif statements following it, and if you want to be sure that at least one clause is executed, you close the structure with an else statement (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nThe chain expresses a decision with many outcomes in a way that guarantees there is only one result. In plain English, the textbook says, it means: if the first condition is true, do this; else, if the second condition is true, do that; otherwise, do something else (Sweigart, 2025). Chaining is cleaner than a series of nested decisions, and it states the order of the tests.\n\nA ticket price that depends on age is a typical chain: one clause for children, one for adults and a closing else clause for seniors. Any classification of a value into ranges, or a command that can be one of several words, has the same shape.\n\nPython tests the conditions in the order written and runs the block of the first one that is true; it does not evaluate the later conditions (Python Software Foundation, 2026). A chain is different from a sequence of separate if statements. With age = 70, two separate statements testing age > 12 and age > 65 both run and print A and B, whereas the same tests joined by elif print only A.\n\nOverlapping conditions make the order important, so a chain should be read from the top. A chain without else may run no block, which the textbook states as only one or none of the clauses will be executed (Sweigart, 2025). When a variable is assigned in the blocks of a chain, a missing else can leave it unassigned, a bug that the worked programs of this chapter demonstrate." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_Branching ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#BranchChain> .
chd:S_ExpressionForm a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Expression form"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Expression form" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 44 ;
    skos:definition "An expression form is a construct that brings a choice or a naming into a single expression, so that it has a value and can be used inside larger expressions. The chapter has two: the conditional expression, which chooses between two values, and the assignment expression, which gives a name to a value while it is being used. Both are described in the language reference as expressions, in contrast with the statements of the previous branches (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nA statement cannot be placed inside a larger expression, but a choice sometimes has to be. A message that depends on a condition may be needed as an argument of a function, or a value may be needed twice in one condition. The two forms of this branch make that possible without splitting the line into several statements.\n\nThe conditional expression is the older and more common of the two. The assignment expression arrived in Python 3.8 and appears mostly in conditions. Both are used sparingly, because a long expression is harder to read than the statements it replaces.\n\nBoth forms are expressions, so both have a value that can be used on the spot. The conditional expression 'child' if age < 12 else 'adult' evaluates to 'child' for an age of 10 and can be passed straight to print, where an if statement could not (Python Software Foundation, 2026). The assignment expression both names and yields: the condition (n := len('Alice')) > 3 evaluates to True and leaves n bound to 5, so the length is computed once and is still available inside the block." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_FlowControl ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#ExpressionForm> .
chd:S_ConditionalExpression a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Conditional expression"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Conditional expression" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 45 ;
    skos:definition "A conditional expression, sometimes called a ternary operator because it combines three operands, is an alternative to the if-else statement. Because it is an expression, it returns a value and can appear as a sub-expression (Python Software Foundation, 2026). It is written x if C else y, where C is a condition and x and y are the two possible values.\n\nChoosing between two values is so common that a statement of four lines is out of proportion. A conditional expression keeps the choice on the line where the value is needed, for example in an assignment or inside a call. It also shows clearly that the whole construct produces one value.\n\nA typical use gives a variable one of two values: 'even' if 10 % 2 == 0 else 'odd' evaluates to 'even', and with 7 in place of 10 it evaluates to 'odd'. It is the right form when both branches are simple values and the choice is small.\n\nPython first evaluates the condition C, not x. If C is true, x is evaluated and its value is returned; otherwise y is evaluated and its value is returned (Python Software Foundation, 2026). Only one of the two values is evaluated, so 'adult' if 20 >= 18 else 1 / 0 evaluates to 'adult' without dividing by zero. In the precedence table the conditional expression comes near the bottom, below the Boolean operators, so a condition does not need parentheses.\n\nThe conditional expression is not a replacement for the if statement. It chooses values, not actions, so each part must be an expression; Python 3.14 reports a statement such as pass in place of a value with the message expected expression after 'else', but statement is given, where Python 3.12 says only invalid syntax (Python Software Foundation, 2026). Nesting several conditional expressions quickly becomes unreadable, and then a normal if statement is clearer." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_ExpressionForm ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#ConditionalExpression> .
chd:S_AssignmentExpression a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Assignment expression"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Assignment expression" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 46 ;
    skos:definition "An assignment expression, also called a named expression or, informally, the walrus operator (the symbol := looks like the eyes and tusks of a walrus), assigns an expression to an identifier while also returning the value of the expression (Python Software Foundation, 2026). It is written with the operator :=. It was added in Python 3.8 by PEP 572, whose formal name for the construct is assignment expressions (Angelico et al., 2018).\n\nNaming the result of an expression is an important part of programming, because it allows a descriptive name to be used in place of a longer expression and permits reuse (Angelico et al., 2018). Until the new form existed, naming a value required a separate assignment statement and was impossible inside a condition.\n\nThe reference documentation shows it in the conditions of if and while statements, where a value is computed, tested and then kept for use inside the block (Python Software Foundation, 2026). A small case in this chapter's terms is a condition that needs a length twice: the line if (size := len(name)) > 3: tests the length and also keeps it in the name size for the block.\n\nThe expression (limit := 10) > 5 assigns 10 to limit and then compares the value with 5, so it evaluates to True and limit remains available afterwards. With name = 'Alice', the statement if (size := len(name)) > 3: followed by print(size) prints 5. The reference documentation says that assignment expressions must be surrounded by parentheses when used as expression statements and in several other places, but that parentheses are not required in if and while statements (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nThe operator := is not a second form of =. As a statement on its own, x := 3 is rejected with the message invalid syntax, and the plain assignment statement is the right tool for naming a value on a line of its own. Overusing the form makes conditions harder to read, so it is best kept for places where the value is needed immediately." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_ExpressionForm ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#AssignmentExpression> .
chd:S_ModernPractice a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Modern practice"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Modern practice" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 47 ;
    skos:definition "Modern practice is the way Python programmers work today: the constructs that current Python offers beyond the textbook and the conventions that experienced developers follow when they write conditions. An advanced course teaches this practice next to the basics of the book, so that students do not have to unlearn habits later.\n\nThe textbook teaches decisions with if, elif and else, and that is complete enough for every program of its chapter. Python 3.10 nevertheless added a statement for choosing among patterns, and the official style guide has firm views on how conditions should be written. A student who knows only the book writes code that works but looks dated to other programmers.\n\nThe match statement is met in code written for Python 3.10 or later, in the documentation and in the release notes. The style guide, PEP 8, is a process document whose status is Active, and it is the reference to turn to whenever a programmer asks how a piece of Python should be written (Van Rossum et al., 2001).\n\nTwo branches follow. Pattern matching explains the match statement and its parts: the case patterns, the wildcard, sequence patterns, guards and the soft keywords that make the new statement possible. Style collects the recommendations of PEP 8 that concern the ideas of this chapter, namely how to compare Boolean values and singletons, how to test for emptiness and how to indent.\n\nModern practice does not replace the basics. A match statement is not a better if statement; it is a different tool for a particular shape of problem, and a style recommendation is a convention that a team may adapt. The advice of this branch is therefore given with the reasons that the sources give for it." ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#ModernPractice> .
chd:S_PatternMatching a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Pattern matching"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Pattern matching" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 48 ;
    skos:definition "Pattern matching is a way of choosing between alternatives by comparing a value with patterns that describe its shape. Python 3.10 added it as the match statement, specified in PEP 634 and explained for learners in PEP 636 (Bucher and van Rossum, 2020) (Moisset, 2020). A pattern can be a plain value, but it can also describe a sequence of a given length, and it can capture parts of the value into names.\n\nA long elif chain that compares one value with many constants is repetitive: the value is named in every condition. A match statement names it once and lists the cases below it. The tutorial recommends the statement when the same value is compared with several constants, or when specific types are checked (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nThe sections of this branch go from the whole to the parts. The match statement shows the structure and the order in which the cases are tried. The case pattern, wildcard pattern and sequence pattern describe the three kinds of pattern that suffice for commands and status codes. The guard adds a condition to a pattern, and the soft keyword explains why match and case can be new words without breaking old programs.\n\nThe cases are tried from the top, and the first pattern that fits wins, so a case of the wildcard at the end catches everything left over (Python Software Foundation, 2026). A pattern binds as well as tests: matching the value [1, 2] against a sequence pattern of two names leaves the first bound to 1 and the second to 2, which is how one pattern replaces a length test and two index expressions. A value that no case matches simply falls through, so a match statement without a wildcard can do nothing at all and report nothing either." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_ModernPractice ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#PatternMatching> .
chd:S_MatchStatement a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Match statement"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Match statement" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 49 ;
    skos:definition "A match statement takes an expression and compares its value with successive patterns given as one or more case blocks. The tutorial says that this is superficially similar to a switch statement in C, Java or JavaScript, but more similar to pattern matching in languages like Rust or Haskell (Python Software Foundation, 2026). Only the first pattern that matches is executed, and if no case matches, none of the branches runs. The statement was added in Python 3.10 (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nThe statement is made for the situation in which one value, such as a command, a status code or a pair of coordinates, determines what happens next. PEP 636 motivates it with a text adventure in which the user types commands and the program must interpret them (Moisset, 2020). Written with elif, each case repeats the name of the value; written with match, the cases line up.\n\nThe tutorial's examples handle status codes, points and colours, and PEP 636 builds the command interpreter of a text adventure with it (Python Software Foundation, 2026) (Moisset, 2020). The Python documentation describes the statement in the language reference and in the tutorial, and the release notes of Python 3.10 announce it as PEP 634 (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nThe reference documentation describes the flow. The subject expression after match is evaluated once. Each pattern in a case block is then attempted against the subject value in order, and the first pattern that succeeds, and whose guard if any is true, has its block executed (Python Software Foundation, 2026). With command = 'quit' and the cases 'go', 'quit' and _, the program prints bye. Names that a successful pattern binds outlive the block and can be used after the match statement.\n\nIf no case matches and there is no wildcard, nothing happens and no error is raised: with the command 'dance' and a single case 'go', the program continues with the line after the statement. The statement also needs Python 3.10 or later. Finally, a bare name in a case is not a comparison but a capture, which the section on case patterns explains." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_PatternMatching ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#MatchStatement> .
chd:S_CasePattern a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Case pattern"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Case pattern" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 50 ;
    skos:definition "A case pattern is the description that follows the word case. According to the reference documentation, pattern matching takes a pattern as input, following case, and a subject value, following match; the pattern, which may contain subpatterns (patterns inside a pattern), is matched against the subject value, and the outcome is a success or a failure and possibly the binding of matched values to names (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nPatterns are what make the statement more than a row of comparisons. A literal pattern checks a value, a capture pattern keeps a part of the value, and an OR pattern, written with vertical bars, accepts several alternatives in one case, so that related cases share a block.\n\nThe tutorial's example separates the status codes 400, 404 and 418, and groups 401, 403 and 404 with the pattern 401 | 403 | 404 (Python Software Foundation, 2026). In a program that sets status = 403 and matches it against the cases 400, 401 | 403 | 404 and _, the block of the second case runs and the program prints not allowed.\n\nA literal pattern, a value written out in the code such as 401 or 'quit', succeeds when the subject is equal to the literal, except for None, True and False, which are compared with is (Python Software Foundation, 2026). A capture pattern is a name; it always succeeds and sets the name to the subject, which is called binding the name. An OR pattern tries its alternatives in turn and succeeds as soon as one does (Python Software Foundation, 2026). When a pattern succeeds, the names it bound outlive the executed block.\n\nA name in a case is a capture and not a comparison. With expected = 'quit', command = 'go' and a single case expected:, the pattern succeeds anyway and sets expected to 'go', so the program prints matched go and has overwritten the variable. To compare with a stored constant, the tutorial says the constant must be a dotted name, such as Color.RED or math.pi, to keep it from being read as a capture variable (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_PatternMatching ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#CasePattern> .
chd:S_WildcardPattern a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Wildcard pattern"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Wildcard pattern" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 51 ;
    skos:definition "The wildcard pattern is written with a single underscore. The reference documentation says that a wildcard pattern always succeeds, matching anything, and binds no name (Python Software Foundation, 2026). The tutorial describes the underscore as a variable name that acts as a wildcard and never fails to match.\n\nA match statement does nothing when no case matches, which is rarely what a program wants for an unknown command. The wildcard provides the case for everything else, in the way that the else clause does in a chain of if and elif clauses.\n\nThe wildcard is the last case of the tutorial's status example, which returns a message for any status that the other cases do not know, and it closes the command example of this section: with the command 'dance' the program prints unknown.\n\nA case block that cannot fail is called irrefutable. The reference allows at most one irrefutable case block in a match statement and requires it to be the last (Python Software Foundation, 2026). Python enforces the rule when it reads the program: a wildcard case followed by another case is rejected with the SyntaxError message wildcard makes remaining patterns unreachable, which means that the later cases could never be used.\n\nThe underscore is special only inside patterns. The reference says that it is a soft keyword within any pattern, but that outside patterns it is an identifier as usual (Python Software Foundation, 2026). A capture pattern with an ordinary name has the same effect of matching everything and, in addition, binds the name, so a name in the last position keeps the value that the wildcard throws away." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_PatternMatching ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#WildcardPattern> .
chd:S_SequencePattern a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Sequence pattern"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Sequence pattern" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 52 ;
    skos:definition "A sequence pattern describes a sequence by listing patterns for its items. The reference documentation states that it contains several subpatterns to be matched against sequence elements, and that the syntax is similar to the unpacking of a list or tuple, that is, the assignment of its items to several names in one statement; there is no difference whether parentheses or square brackets are used (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nCommands usually arrive as a few words, and the program must know how many words it received before it can use them. PEP 636 starts from the observation that unpacking a command into an action and an object fails with an error if the user types more or fewer words than two (Moisset, 2020). A sequence pattern checks the length and takes the parts in one step.\n\nThe pattern is used for commands such as ['go', direction] and ['quit'], for coordinate pairs such as (x, y), and for any value that has a fixed shape. The tutorial notes that tuple and list patterns have exactly the same meaning and match arbitrary sequences, with the exception that they do not match strings (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nFor a fixed-length pattern the subject must be a sequence of the same length, and the subpatterns are matched with the items from left to right, stopping at the first failure (Python Software Foundation, 2026). With command = ['go', 'north'], the case ['go', direction] succeeds, binds direction to 'north', and the program prints going north. A star subpattern such as *rest matches a list of the remaining items (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nA string is not matched by a sequence pattern, even though a string is a sequence of characters. The reference says that if the subject is a string or a sequence of bytes the sequence pattern fails (Python Software Foundation, 2026): matching 'ab' against the case [a, b] fails, and a program with a wildcard case prints no match." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_PatternMatching ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#SequencePattern> .
chd:S_Guard a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Guard"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Guard" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 53 ;
    skos:definition "A guard is an extra condition that is attached to a case. It takes the form of the word if followed by an expression, and the reference documentation says that it must succeed for the code inside the case block to execute (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nPatterns can describe the shape and the constants of a value, but not every relationship between its parts. A guard supplies the rest, for example that the two coordinates of a point are equal, which a pattern alone cannot say.\n\nThe tutorial uses a guard to separate the points that lie on a diagonal from the others (Python Software Foundation, 2026). The same idea can be written for a pair: the case (x, y) if x == y selects the pair (3, 3), and the case (x, y) that follows it handles every other pair.\n\nPython first checks the pattern. If it fails, the guard is not evaluated and the next case block is checked. If it succeeds, the names are bound and the guard is evaluated: a true guard selects the case block, a false guard does not, and the next case is tried (Python Software Foundation, 2026). The tutorial points out that value capture happens before the guard is evaluated (Python Software Foundation, 2026). For the pair (3, 3) the program prints on the diagonal at 3, and for (3, 4) it prints elsewhere.\n\nA guard belongs to the case line and is not an if statement. If the guard raises an exception, it propagates, and guards are evaluated in order, one case at a time, until a case block is selected (Python Software Foundation, 2026). Do not rely on a guard to run, because a case whose pattern fails never reaches its guard." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_PatternMatching ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#Guard> .
chd:S_SoftKeyword a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Soft keyword"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Soft keyword" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 54 ;
    skos:definition "A soft keyword is a name that is reserved only in specific contexts. The language reference lists match, case and _ as soft keywords when they are used in the match statement, and it adds that the distinction is made by the parser and not when the text is split into tokens (Python Software Foundation, 2026). A further soft keyword, type, belongs to a statement that this course does not use. The concept was added in Python 3.10 together with the match statement.\n\nSoft keywords are a compatibility measure. As the reference puts it, they make it possible to extend the grammar while preserving compatibility with existing code that uses these names as identifier names (Python Software Foundation, 2026). Without them, adding the match statement would have broken every program that had a variable called match.\n\nThe standard library module keyword can tell the two kinds of word apart. The call keyword.iskeyword('match') evaluates to False, while keyword.issoftkeyword('match') evaluates to True, and keyword.iskeyword('if') evaluates to True (Python Software Foundation, 2026). PEP 634 explains that match and case are recognised as keywords when they are part of a match statement or a case block only (Bucher and van Rossum, 2020).\n\nOutside a match statement the words are ordinary names. A program may assign match = 3 and case = 4 and then print match + case, which prints 7. Inside a match statement the parser reads the same words as keywords, so there is no clash between the two uses.\n\nThat a word is allowed as a name does not make it a good name. A reader who sees a variable called match in the neighbourhood of a match statement has to work out which meaning applies, so the names are best avoided even though Python permits them." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_PatternMatching ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#SoftKeyword> .
chd:S_Style a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Style"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Style" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 55 ;
    skos:definition "Style is the set of conventions for writing code that other people can read easily. For Python the main reference is PEP 8, the style guide for Python code, written by Guido van Rossum, Barry Warsaw and Alyssa Coghlan (Van Rossum et al., 2001). This branch collects the recommendations of PEP 8 that concern the ideas of Chapter 2.\n\nPEP 8 recalls that code is read much more often than it is written (Van Rossum et al., 2001), and readers expect the conventions of their community. A condition written in the recommended way is shorter, and a reviewer can read it without stopping. Style recommendations are also not arbitrary: several of them protect against genuine mistakes, such as the comparison of a value with None.\n\nFour sections follow. The recommendation on Boolean comparison says how to test a Boolean value, the one on singleton comparison says how to test for None, the one on emptiness says how to test a sequence, and the one on indentation says how to lay out blocks. The sections quote PEP 8 and test its claims with short programs.\n\nThe recommendations can be tested instead of being taken on authority. For a real Boolean the condition greeting == True and the plain condition greeting agree, since True == True and False == True give the value back unchanged; for every other value they part company, because bool('False') is True while 'False' == True is False (Van Rossum et al., 2001). The same guide asks that a comparison with None use is or is not rather than the equality operators, and that an empty sequence be tested with not before the name rather than by comparing its length with zero." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_ModernPractice ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#Style> .
chd:S_BooleanComparisonStyle a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Boolean comparison style"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Boolean comparison style" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 56 ;
    skos:definition "PEP 8 recommends that a Boolean value not be compared to True or False with the equality operator. Its example reads: correct is if greeting:, wrong is if greeting == True:, and the form if greeting is True: is called worse (Van Rossum et al., 2001).\n\nA condition is already a truth value, so comparing it with True adds a comparison without adding information. The longer form is also less safe, because it tests something different from what its author usually means: with greeting = 'hello', the condition greeting == True is false, because the string is not equal to True, while the condition greeting is true, because a non-empty string counts as true.\n\nThe textbook's own Opposite Day program contains conditions such as if today_is_opposite_day == True: (Sweigart, 2025). The program is correct, and a reviewer who follows PEP 8 would write if today_is_opposite_day: instead. The same applies to conditions that come out of comparisons: they can be used as they are.\n\nThe comparison with True is redundant for a real Boolean value, because True == True evaluates to True and False == True evaluates to False, so the result equals the value itself. For other values the comparison changes the meaning, as in the example above. The plain form lets Python apply the rule of truthiness (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nThe recommendation concerns conditions, not every use of a Boolean value: a program may still store, print and pass on True and False. If a program really must know that a value is exactly True and not merely true, it has to say so in its own words, and the reader should be told why." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_Style ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#BooleanComparisonStyle> .
chd:S_SingletonComparison a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Singleton comparison"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Singleton comparison" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 57 ;
    skos:definition "The style guide says that comparisons to singletons like None should always be done with is or is not, never the equality operators (Van Rossum et al., 2001). The reference documentation repeats the advice when it describes None as a singleton (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nThe reason lies in how the operators work. Equality can be customised by the kind of object that is compared, whereas the behaviour of is cannot be customised and it never raises an exception (Python Software Foundation, 2026). Asking whether a value is the one and only None is a question of identity, and the identity operator answers exactly that.\n\nThe test appears wherever a variable may hold the absence of a value, for example before a result is used. PEP 8 also gives the preferred spelling of the negative test: if foo is not None: is correct and if not foo is None: is wrong, because the two forms are equivalent but the first is more readable (Van Rossum et al., 2001).\n\nWith x = None, the expressions x is None and x is not None evaluate to True and False. The test asks only whether the value is the object None, and it does not look at the truth of the value: with x = [], x is not None evaluates to True although bool(x) is False.\n\nPEP 8 warns against writing if x when the intention is if x is not None. The variable might have been set to some other value that counts as false, such as an empty container, and the shorter test would treat it as unset (Van Rossum et al., 2001). Choose between the plain truth test and the test against None according to the question that the program is really asking." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_Style ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#SingletonComparison> .
chd:S_EmptinessTest a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Emptiness test"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Emptiness test" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 58 ;
    skos:definition "PEP 8 advises that, for sequences such as strings, lists and tuples, the program should use the fact that empty sequences are false. Its example shows if not seq: and if seq: as correct and if len(seq): and if not len(seq): as wrong (Van Rossum et al., 2001).\n\nThe plain test says what is meant: does the sequence have content. It also works for every kind of sequence without asking for its length, and it reads like the English sentence it stands for. Python itself defines the truth of a sequence in this way, so the shorter test is not a trick but the documented rule (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nThe test appears when a program has collected something and must decide whether there is anything to process, for example whether the user typed a name or a search found results. The rule applies equally to strings, lists and tuples.\n\nThe documentation lists the empty string, the empty tuple and the empty list among the values that are considered false (Python Software Foundation, 2026). The two forms of the test therefore agree: bool([]) evaluates to False and len([]) evaluates to 0, and bool([1, 2]) evaluates to True and len([1, 2]) evaluates to 2.\n\nThe truth test cannot distinguish an empty sequence from the value None, since both are false. When the difference matters, test for None separately with is, as the section on singleton comparison explains. Remember also that the rule is about emptiness: a string that holds a space, or a list that holds a zero, is not empty." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_Style ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#EmptinessTest> .
chd:S_IndentationStyle a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Indentation style"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Indentation style" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 59 ;
    skos:definition "The style guide's rules about indentation are short. It says to use four spaces per indentation level, that spaces are the preferred indentation method, that tabs should be used solely to remain consistent with code that is already indented with tabs, and that Python disallows mixing tabs and spaces for indentation (Van Rossum et al., 2001).\n\nBecause Python reads indentation as structure, a consistent habit prevents a whole class of errors that come from lines that look aligned but are not. Four spaces are wide enough to show the structure at a glance, and programmers who follow the same rule find each other's code familiar.\n\nThe convention holds for the examples of this chapter: the textbook indents the block of each if, elif and else clause by four spaces (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nA line that is indented by one level more than the line above it opens a block, and a line that is indented by one level less closes one. The style guide also discourages compound statements, which put several statements on one line: if foo == 'blah': do_blah_thing() is called wrong, and the same is said for an else clause that is written on the same line as its suite (Van Rossum et al., 2001).\n\nPython accepts other amounts of indentation than four, and it only insists that they are used consistently. A program that happens to run is therefore not necessarily a program in good style. Mixing tabs and spaces is the one choice that the language itself may refuse to read." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_Style ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#IndentationStyle> .
chd:S_WorkedProgram a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Worked program"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Worked program" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 60 ;
    skos:definition "A worked program is a complete short program in which the ideas of a chapter are used together. Chapter 2 ends with two of them. Opposite Day uses if and else statements to set a Boolean variable and to reverse it, and the Dishonest Capacity Calculator uses an if and an elif clause to choose a conversion factor according to the unit that the user types (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nIsolated examples show one idea at a time, whereas a program shows how the ideas depend on each other: a comparison feeds a condition, the condition selects a block, the block assigns a variable and a later line uses it. Reading complete programs is the skill that the chapter has been preparing, and the second program also shows a typical bug that grows out of a missing branch.\n\nThe programs are saved as oppositeday.py and dishonestcapacity.py in the textbook, and they are the last examples before the summary and the practice questions (Sweigart, 2025). The first is a puzzle in logic, the second a small useful calculation.\n\nThe branch has one sub-branch, decision patterns, which pulls four patterns out of the programs: toggling a Boolean variable, accepting two spellings of one answer, noticing that a variable may never have been assigned, and guarding against input that no branch accepts.\n\nThe textbook is frank about the quality of its programs. It remarks that the Opposite Day program could be replaced by a single print and that the capacity program fails for an invalid unit (Sweigart, 2025). The programs are teaching devices, and learners should read them critically." ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#WorkedProgram> .
chd:S_DecisionPattern a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Decision pattern"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Decision pattern" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 61 ;
    skos:definition "A decision pattern is a way of using the flow control statements that recurs in many programs. The two worked programs of the textbook (Sweigart, 2025) contain four: the toggle, which reverses a Boolean variable; the case-insensitive test, which accepts several spellings of one answer; the unassigned name, which is a bug that appears when no branch runs; and the input guard, which is the cure for that bug.\n\nPatterns turn single statements into reusable solutions. A programmer who has seen the toggle once recognises it in every later program, and one who has seen the unassigned name once checks every chain of conditions for a missing else clause.\n\nThe section on each pattern takes the textbook's program as its example and checks its output by running it. The first two patterns are things to do, and the last two belong together as a problem and its remedy.\n\nThe last two patterns are a fault and its cure, and the fault can be produced on demand. A chain of if and elif clauses with no else leaves its variable unbound whenever no condition holds, so the next line stops the program with NameError: name 'answer' is not defined (Python Software Foundation, 2026). The input guard is the cure: a final else clause, or a loop that asks again, so that every path through the chain binds the name. The toggle needs only one line by comparison, since spam = not spam reverses a Boolean in place." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_WorkedProgram ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#DecisionPattern> .
chd:S_Toggle a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Toggle"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Toggle" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 62 ;
    skos:definition "To toggle a Boolean variable is to set it to its opposite Boolean value (Sweigart, 2025). In Python the idiom assigns the negation of the variable to the variable itself: flag = not flag. The textbook uses it in the Opposite Day program, where the line say_it_is_opposite_day = not say_it_is_opposite_day reverses the answer when today is Opposite Day.\n\nMany programs keep a switch that is on or off: a light, a mode, a player whose turn it is. The toggle changes such a switch without asking what its present state is, which makes the code shorter and removes one decision.\n\nThe Opposite Day program of the textbook has three if statements. The first sets say_it_is_opposite_day to True when today_is_opposite_day is True and to False otherwise. The second toggles the variable if today is Opposite Day. The third prints a message according to the variable (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nWith today_is_opposite_day set to True, the first statement sets the other variable to True, the second reverses it to False, and the third prints Today is not Opposite Day. With the first line changed to False, the first statement sets the variable to False, the second is skipped, and the program prints the same message (Sweigart, 2025). A short test shows the idiom: with flag = True, the line flag = not flag leaves False in flag.\n\nThe textbook points out that the program can never print Today is Opposite Day, whatever value the first variable has, and that the whole program could be replaced by one print statement (Sweigart, 2025). A sequence of decisions can cancel itself out, and the way to find out is to follow every path, as a flowchart does. The conditions of the program also compare a Boolean value with True, which the style guide advises against." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_DecisionPattern ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#Toggle> .
chd:S_CaseInsensitiveTest a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Case-insensitive test"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Case-insensitive test" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 63 ;
    skos:definition "A case-insensitive test is a condition that accepts an answer whether it is typed in uppercase or in lowercase. The textbook builds it from the Boolean operator or: the if and elif statements of the Dishonest Capacity Calculator use or so that the program works no matter whether the user enters the unit in lowercase or uppercase (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nUsers type the same answer in different ways, and a program that accepts only one of them seems broken to everyone else. A test that covers both spellings costs one more comparison and saves the user an error message.\n\nThe program asks for the unit in which a drive is advertised and tests unit == 'TB' or unit == 'tb' and then unit == 'GB' or unit == 'gb'. For the first branch it computes the factor 1000000000000 / 1099511627776, which evaluates to 0.9094947017729282, and for the second 1000000000 / 1073741824, which evaluates to 0.9313225746154785 (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nEach side of the or is a complete comparison, and the whole condition is true if either comparison is. For an advertised capacity of 10 TB the real capacity is 10 multiplied by the factor, which is 9.094947017729282, and round(9.094947017729282, 2) gives 9.09. The function str turns that number into the text '9.09', so that it can be joined to the message with a plus sign (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nThe test lists the accepted spellings and no others. A user who types Tb, with a capital T and a lowercase b, matches neither comparison, so 'Tb' == 'TB' or 'Tb' == 'tb' evaluates to False and no branch runs. Writing each spelling as a full comparison is also necessary, since unit == 'TB' or 'tb' is true for every unit." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_DecisionPattern ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#CaseInsensitiveTest> .
chd:S_UnassignedName a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Unassigned name"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Unassigned name" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 64 ;
    skos:definition "An unassigned name is a variable that the program uses without any assignment to it having been executed. In the Dishonest Capacity Calculator the variable discrepancy is assigned inside the if clause and the elif clause only. If the user enters a unit that is neither TB, tb, GB nor gb, neither clause runs and the variable is never created (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nThe bug is typical of decisions without an alternative. The program does not fail at the decision, where the cause lies, but later at a line that uses the variable, and by then the connection to the decision is hard to see. The textbook notes that the user would not know that anything was wrong until Python tried to use the nonexistent variable (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nAny chain of if and elif clauses that assigns a variable in each of its blocks, and has no else clause, can leave the variable unassigned. The situation is common when the program reads input, because the user can type anything.\n\nWhen the program reaches the line that uses the variable, Python looks for the name, does not find it, and raises a NameError with the message name 'discrepancy' is not defined. The textbook says that the error points to the line where real_capacity is assigned, which is the line that first uses the variable, although the true origin lies in the decision (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nThe remedy is to make sure that the variable always receives a value, or that the program stops when it cannot. The next section shows the second approach with an else clause. A third approach, which is not used in the textbook, is to give the variable a first value before the chain begins." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_DecisionPattern ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#UnassignedName> .
chd:S_InputGuard a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Input guard"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Input guard" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 65 ;
    skos:definition "An input guard is a branch that deals with input which none of the expected cases accept. The textbook identifies the true origin of the unassigned variable as the fact that the program does not handle the case where the user enters an invalid unit, and says that the simplest remedy is an else clause that displays a message such as You must enter TB or GB and then calls the sys.exit() function to quit the program (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nA guard changes a confusing failure into a clear one. Instead of a NameError that mentions a variable the user has never heard of, the user is told what to type. For the programmer, the guard states in the code which inputs the program is prepared to handle, and the else clause is the natural place for all the others.\n\nThe function sys.exit belongs to the module sys, which a program imports with the statement import sys, as Chapter 1 explained for packages. The textbook defers a full description of the function to the next chapter (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nThe documentation says that sys.exit raises a SystemExit exception, signalling an intention to exit the interpreter. If it is given a string, the string is printed to the error output and the exit status, the number that a finished program hands back to the system, is 1, and the documentation calls sys.exit with an error message a quick way to exit a program when an error occurs (Python Software Foundation, 2026). In the capacity program, with the unit 'KB', the else clause runs, the message You must enter TB or GB is shown and the program ends before the variable is used.\n\nThe guard must come last, after all the accepted cases, because else takes everything that is left. It also does not replace checking the number the user types: the textbook converts the advertised capacity with float, which would fail for text that is not a number. A guard handles the cases the programmer thought of, and thinking of them is the real work." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_DecisionPattern ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#InputGuard> .
chd:S_ErrorReport a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Error report"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Error report" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 66 ;
    skos:definition "An error report is what Python prints when it cannot carry out a line of a program. The report names the kind of error and gives a message about it, and for errors that happen while the program runs it also shows the lines through which execution arrived. The chapter meets four kinds of error: SyntaxError, NameError, TypeError and ZeroDivisionError (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nReading the report correctly is the first skill of debugging, the work of finding and removing the cause of a fault in a program. The tutorial notes that the last line of an error message indicates what happened and that the type of the exception is printed as part of the message (Python Software Foundation, 2026). A learner who reads that line, instead of looking only at the length of the report, finds the cause much faster.\n\nThe textbook shows error reports when it enters true, when it assigns to False, and when the capacity program uses an unassigned variable (Sweigart, 2025). The examples of this branch add the reports that a learner is likely to see while practising the chapter: a missing colon, a bad indentation, a comparison between a number and a string, and a division by zero.\n\nThe branch has one sub-branch, the kinds of error, with a section for each kind and one for the traceback, the report as a whole. Every message quoted in the branch was produced by running the line in question, because the wording of messages changes between releases.\n\nThe message wording is not a stable part of the language. Python 3.14 words some messages more helpfully than Python 3.12 does, as the section on the else clause showed for an elif that follows an else, so the kind of error is more reliable than its message." ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#ErrorReport> .
chd:S_ErrorKind a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Kind of error"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Kind of error" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 67 ;
    skos:definition "A kind of error is the type that Python prints at the start of the last line of an error report. The tutorial distinguishes syntax errors, which the parser finds when it reads the text, from exceptions, which are errors detected during execution in a statement or expression that is syntactically correct (Python Software Foundation, 2026). The kinds seen in this chapter are SyntaxError, with its subclass IndentationError, NameError, TypeError and ZeroDivisionError.\n\nEach kind points to a different class of cause. A SyntaxError says that the text of the program is wrong, a NameError that a name has no value, a TypeError that an operation was applied to values of the wrong types, and a ZeroDivisionError that a number was divided by zero. Knowing the family narrows the search before the message has been read.\n\nThe sections of this branch define each kind with the wording of the documentation, show a line from the chapter that produces it and report the message that the line really produces. The section on the traceback then explains the report as a whole.\n\nThe report names the kind on its last line, and every kind in this chapter can be produced in a single line. The expression 1 / 0 raises ZeroDivisionError: division by zero, joining a string to a number raises a TypeError saying that only a str may be concatenated to a str, reading a name before any assignment raises NameError: name 'spam' is not defined, and a header such as if spam = 42: is refused before the program starts with a SyntaxError (Python Software Foundation, 2026). Reading the kind first and the message second is the shortest route from the report to the cause." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_ErrorReport ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#ErrorKind> .
chd:S_Exception a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Exception"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Exception" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 68 ;
    skos:definition "An exception is an error that is detected while a program runs. The tutorial explains that even if a statement or expression is syntactically correct, it may cause an error when an attempt is made to execute it, and that errors detected during execution are called exceptions and are not unconditionally fatal (Python Software Foundation, 2026). A program can be written to handle an exception, a topic that comes later in the course; most exceptions are not handled and result in an error report.\n\nThe word separates two moments at which a line can fail. A syntax error is found when the program text is read, before anything runs, whereas an exception arises only when execution reaches the line. This explains why a faulty line inside a block that is skipped does not disturb a program that never executes it.\n\nThe NameError, TypeError and ZeroDivisionError of this chapter are exceptions, and so is the SystemExit that the function sys.exit raises. Each report names the exception: the tutorial's examples show ZeroDivisionError, NameError and TypeError, and the type printed is the name of the built-in exception that occurred (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nWhen an exception is raised, Python stops the statement that raised it, looks for code that handles it, and prints the error report if none is found. The last line of the report gives the exception's type and the rest of the line gives details that depend on the type and the cause (Python Software Foundation, 2026). The built-in exception names are ordinary built-in names and not reserved keywords (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nAn exception is not a syntax error and cannot be found by reading the text alone. The same line can run in one execution and raise an exception in another, depending on the data. The exception SystemExit is a special case: it is raised on purpose to end the program, and the interpreter then exits without printing a traceback (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_ErrorKind ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#Exception> .
chd:S_SyntaxErrorKind a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "SyntaxError"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "SyntaxError" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 69 ;
    skos:definition "A SyntaxError is raised when the parser, the part of Python that reads the text of a program, encounters a syntax error, that is, text that does not follow the grammar, the set of rules that say how correct Python text is written (Python Software Foundation, 2026). The tutorial calls such errors parsing errors and says that they are perhaps the most common kind of complaint while one is still learning the language. An IndentationError is a subclass of SyntaxError (a kind of error defined as a more specific case of another kind) for errors in the indentation, and a TabError is a subclass of that for the inconsistent use of tabs and spaces (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nBecause the parser reads the whole text of a program before anything runs, a SyntaxError stops the program at once, and none of it is executed. It is the first kind of error that a learner meets, and it is also the kind whose report is the most helpful: it shows the offending line and arrows that point to the place where the error was detected.\n\nThe chapter's examples produce it in several ways. The line False = 2 + 2 gives cannot assign to False; the line if = 3 gives invalid syntax; the header if True without its colon gives expected ':'; and the condition of an if statement written with a single equals sign gives a message that suggests ==. An else clause with a condition gives invalid syntax.\n\nThe parser reports the place where it noticed the problem. The tutorial adds that this is not always the place that needs to be fixed: in its example the error is detected at the function print, because a colon is missing just before it (Python Software Foundation, 2026). A missing colon, a missing quotation mark or a wrong operator are the most frequent causes.\n\nDo not stop at the first message. When the reported line looks correct, look at the lines before it, because an unclosed bracket is often noticed only at a later line. The wording of the message differs between versions of Python: an elif written after an else clause is reported in Python 3.14 as 'elif' block follows an 'else' block, and in Python 3.12 only as invalid syntax." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_ErrorKind ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#SyntaxErrorKind> .
chd:S_NameErrorKind a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "NameError"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "NameError" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 70 ;
    skos:definition "A NameError is raised when a local or global name is not found. The documentation adds that the associated value is an error message that includes the name that could not be found (Python Software Foundation, 2026). The message therefore always has the same shape: name, followed by the missing name in quotation marks, followed by is not defined.\n\nA name in Python exists only after it has been assigned, so any use of a name before its assignment, or of a name that was spelled differently, fails in this way. The error is therefore a precise report about the program: it names the exact word that Python could not find.\n\nThe textbook produces it in the interactive shell by entering true in lowercase, which gives name 'true' is not defined (Sweigart, 2025). The tutorial produces it with 4 + spam*3 when spam has no value (Python Software Foundation, 2026). In the capacity program the name discrepancy is missing when the unit is invalid.\n\nWhen Python meets a name in an expression, it looks for a value that was assigned to that name. If none exists, it raises the error at once, even if a similar name does exist, and it does not substitute the similar name. The printed report of the releases used for this chapter does add a hint after the message: for true it ends with Did you mean: 'True'?, while the message of the error itself stays name 'true' is not defined. A name is case sensitive, so true and True are different words, and a name that is assigned only inside a block that did not run is also missing.\n\nThe error is reported where the name is used, not where it should have been assigned. When the cause is a typing mistake the fix is local, but when the name was assigned only inside a branch that did not run, as with discrepancy, the cause is in the logic and the fix is a new branch or a first value." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_ErrorKind ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#NameErrorKind> .
chd:S_TypeErrorKind a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "TypeError"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "TypeError" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 71 ;
    skos:definition "A TypeError is raised when an operation or function is applied to an object of inappropriate type. The associated value is a string giving details about the type mismatch (Python Software Foundation, 2026). It is the error of Chapter 1, where a string and a number were added, and it returns in this chapter when an order comparison is applied to unrelated types.\n\nThe error protects against silent guessing. Python could try to convert the string '4' to a number or the number 3 to a string, but either choice would hide a mistake. The language refuses and reports both types that were involved, which is the information needed to repair the line.\n\nIn this chapter it appears when a number is compared by order with text, as in 3 < '4', which raises a TypeError with the message '<' not supported between instances of 'int' and 'str'. The comparison for equality never raises it, since values of different types are simply unequal.\n\nThe operator < looks at the types of its two operands. For an integer and a string there is no defined order, so Python stops. The reference documentation confirms that the ordering operators are only defined where they make sense (Python Software Foundation, 2026). The message names the operator and the type of each operand.\n\nA TypeError usually means that a conversion is missing. A value that was typed in with input is a string even if it looks like a number, so it must be converted with int or float before it is compared by order with a number. The reverse repair, comparing both as text, is possible but gives the code-point ordering that the section on string ordering described." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_ErrorKind ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#TypeErrorKind> .
chd:S_ZeroDivisionErrorKind a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "ZeroDivisionError"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "ZeroDivisionError" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 72 ;
    skos:definition "A ZeroDivisionError is raised when the second argument of a division or modulo operation (the remainder of a division) is zero. The documentation adds that the associated value is a string indicating the type of the operands and the operation (Python Software Foundation, 2026). For the expression 1 / 0 the message is division by zero.\n\nDivision by zero has no answer, so Python stops the program and says so. The error often appears at the end of a chain of calculations, and the cause lies earlier, in a value that should not have been zero.\n\nThe tutorial's first example of an exception is the line 10 * (1/0) (Python Software Foundation, 2026). In this chapter the error is the thing that short-circuit evaluation can avoid: the guard x != 0 and 10 / x > 2 never divides when x is zero, and the expression 1 or 1 / 0 never reaches its division.\n\nPython evaluates the operands, sees the zero, and raises the error before it produces a result. The remainder operator behaves in the same way, so 1 % 0 raises a ZeroDivisionError too; its message is division by zero in Python 3.14 and integer modulo by zero in Python 3.12. A division in a branch that does not run is not evaluated, so it cannot raise the error.\n\nThe error is not prevented by testing the result, because there is none. The test has to be made on the divisor before the division is carried out, which is what a guarding condition does. Division of a float by 0.0 raises the same error: 1 / 0.0 gives the message division by zero in Python 3.14 and float division by zero in Python 3.12." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_ErrorKind ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#ZeroDivisionErrorKind> .
chd:S_Traceback a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Traceback"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Traceback" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 73 ;
    skos:definition "A traceback is the report that Python prints when an error stops a program. The tutorial explains that the last line of the report indicates what happened, that the rest of that line gives details based on the kind of error, and that the preceding part shows the context where the exception occurred, in the form of a list of the lines of source code through which the program reached the error (Python Software Foundation, 2026).\n\nThe traceback is a map from the symptom to the place. The last line says what went wrong, and the lines above it say where, so that a programmer can go straight to the line that raised the error and to the chain of lines that led to it. The textbook shows the shape of the report in its first examples (Sweigart, 2025).\n\nA traceback appears in the interactive shell after a failed line, and in the output of a program saved in a file when it stops. The capacity program, run with a unit that no branch accepts and without a guard, ends with a traceback whose first line is Traceback (most recent call last): and whose last line is NameError: name 'discrepancy' is not defined.\n\nThe report begins with the line Traceback (most recent call last): and then lists the position in the file, the line number and the source line in which the error arose. Because the most recent call is last, the final entry is the nearest to the cause, and the closing line names the kind of error and its message.\n\nA traceback is printed only when an error is not handled. A program that stops with sys.exit and a message prints the message and no traceback, because the interpreter treats SystemExit as a request to leave and, when it is not handled, exits without printing a traceback (Python Software Foundation, 2026). Read the traceback from the bottom: the last line names the error, and the line above it shows the code that raised it." ;
    doc:hasParentSection chd:S_ErrorKind ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch02#Traceback> .
